window.PHASE_DATA_LN=window.PHASE_DATA_LN||{};window.PHASE_DATA_LN[4]={"pages":[{"pdf":84,"printed":69,"ch":4,"text":"CHAPTER 4\n\nLayer 3: Bank Capital, Credit Risk and\nthe Indian Banking System\nBefore you start: what you need to know first\nLayer 3 is where the thesis stops being about climate and starts being about banks. It has fewer prerequisites than it looks, but the vocabulary is dense and every later layer assumes it. Learn these seven ideas first; the sources in this layer are written for people who already know them.\n\n1. The bank balance sheet\nA bank's assets are mostly loans and investments. Its liabilities are mostly deposits and borrowings. The gap is equity capital, which is the cushion that absorbs losses before depositors are hurt. A bank with a thin cushion relative to its assets is highly leveraged.\n\n2. Risk-weighted assets and the capital ratio\nRegulators do not compare capital with total assets alone. They scale each exposure by a risk weight to get risk-weighted assets (RWA), and then compare capital with RWA: Capital adequacy ratio (CAR) =\n\neligible regulatory capital\nrisk-weighted assets\n\nA riskier loan carries a higher weight, so it needs more capital behind it. This ratio is the dependent variable your thesis is ultimately about: how climate risk moves the numerator (through losses) and the denominator (through higher risk weights or credit deterioration).\n\n3. Tiers of capital\n• Common Equity Tier 1 (CET1): the highest-quality capital, mainly paid-in equity and retained earnings.\n• Additional Tier 1 (AT1): loss-absorbing instruments that can be written off or converted.\n• Tier 2: lower-quality capital that absorbs loss in a gone-concern case.\nThe regulation sets a minimum for each and adds buffers on top. The exact percentages for India are in the RBI section below.\n\n4. Credit-risk parameters: PD, LGD, EAD\n• Probability of default (PD): the chance a borrower defaults within a year."},{"pdf":85,"printed":70,"ch":4,"text":"• Loss given default (LGD): the share of the exposure lost if default happens.\n• Exposure at default (EAD): the amount owed at the time of default.\n\nExpected loss (EL) = PD × LGD × EAD\nExpected loss is meant to be covered by provisions and pricing. Capital is held for unexpected loss, the deviation from the expected value in bad years. A climate shock can raise PD and LGD for exposed borrowers (higher expected loss) and also widen the spread of outcomes (higher unexpected loss). Keep the two apart.\n\n5. Standardised versus internal-ratings approaches\nUnder the standardised approach regulators fix the risk weights by exposure type and external rating. Under the internal ratings-based (IRB) approach, banks estimate PD (and, in the advanced version, LGD and EAD) themselves, subject to supervisory approval. Which one a bank uses determines how credit deterioration reaches its RWA.\n\n6. Asset quality and provisioning\nAn NPA (non-performing asset) is a loan that has stopped paying. Provisions are charges against profit that build a reserve for expected losses. Gross NPA ratios, provisioning coverage and slippages are the standard indicators of asset quality, and they are what the Reserve Bank's annual reports track.\n\n7. The three pillars and the Basel generations\nThe Basel framework has three pillars: Pillar 1 minimum capital for credit, market and operational risk; Pillar 2 supervisory review and a bank-specific capital add-on; Pillar 3 public disclosure. Basel I (1988) introduced risk weights, Basel II (2004) added internal models, and Basel III (after the 2008 crisis) raised the quality and quantity of capital and added buffers and a leverage ratio. India follows Basel III with its own, stricter, thresholds.\n\nMap of Layer 3\nTable 4.1. Before you start: Map of Layer 3\nSection\n\nSource\n\nOne-line job\n\nBorio, Drehmann and Tsatsaronis\n(2014)\n\nWhat macro stress tests can and cannot do, and\nhow to run one honestly.\n\nBasel Framework (BCBS)\n\nThe international rulebook: definitions, ratios,\nbuffers, credit-risk weights.\n\nRBI Basel III Capital Regulations\n(2025)\n\nIndia's version: the actual minimums and the\nweights Indian banks face.\n\nHull, Risk Management and\nFinancial Institutions\n\nThe textbook explanation of how banks measure\nand manage risk, and how capital rules work."},{"pdf":86,"printed":71,"ch":4,"text":"Section\n\nSource\n\nOne-line job\n\nBluhm, Overbeck and Wagner, An\nIntroduction to Credit Risk Modeling\n\nThe mathematics of PD, LGD, default correlation\nand portfolio loss.\n\nRBI Trend and Progress of Banking\nin India\n\nWhat the actual numbers look like for Indian\npublic sector banks, 2008 to 2025.\n\nMaster list\n\nQuotes and how Layer 3 feeds the thesis.\n\nSuggested reading order: Borio (13 pages) first, then the RBI regulations (which are readable and specific), then the Basel Framework as a reference, then Hull and Bluhm only for the chapters that matter to you, then the Trend and Progress reports for the Indian data. How Layer 3 connects to the rest. Layers 1 and 2 give the climate shock and its policy price. Layer 3 gives the bank's capital arithmetic. Layer 4 asks how losses spread between banks. Layers 5 and 6 test how markets and regulators price the whole chain. The climate-tocapital link itself is my framing of the thesis question; the sources in this layer do not mention climate.\n\n1. Borio, Drehmann and Tsatsaronis (2014), Stress-testing macro\nstress testing\nSource: Borio, C., Drehmann, M. and Tsatsaronis, K. (2014), ”Stress-testing macro stress testing: Does it live up to expectations?”, Journal of Financial Stability 12: 3–15 (your file ”Borio, Drehmann & Tsatsaronis (2014).pdf”). The authors are at the Bank for International Settlements. Correction to my map above: this paper is not about the credit-to-GDP gap or capital ratios; it reviews what macro stress tests can and cannot do. That makes it more useful for your thesis than the description I gave. Page references are the journal pages (3 to 15). What I read. The full text, including both annexes. A macro stress test applies a hypothetical adverse scenario to the financial system as a whole to see whether banks keep enough capital. Any climate stress test you build or cite is a member of this family, so the limitations in this paper apply to your own work.\n\nVocabulary\nTable 4.2. Borio, Drehmann and Tsatsaronis (2014): Vocabulary\nTerm\n\nMeaning\n\nMacro stress test\n\nA test of the stability of the financial system, or a part of it, under a\nhypothetical adverse scenario (p. 4).\n\nFour elements\n\nRisk exposures, the scenario (exogenous shocks), the model that maps\nshocks to impact, and a measure of the outcome (p. 4).\n\n”Severe yet plausible”\n\nThe usual rule for scenarios: severe enough to matter, plausible enough to\nbe taken seriously (p. 4).\n\nTop-down / bottom-up\n\nTop-down: the authority runs its own model. Bottom-up: banks run the\nscenario in their own models and the authority aggregates (p. 5)."},{"pdf":87,"printed":72,"ch":4,"text":"Term\n\nMeaning\n\nReverse stress test\n\nStart from a failure outcome and search for the shocks that cause it (p.\n4–5).\n\nParadox of financial\ninstability\n\nThe system looks strongest precisely when it is most vulnerable (p. 8).\n\nFinancial cycle\n\nThe medium-term joint movement of credit and property prices; crises tend\nto begin near its peak (p. 7).\n\nThe argument: five propositions\n1. Macro stress testing is a toolbox, not a single tool (p. 4–5). Tests differ in which institutions and risks they cover, in scenarios, models and outcome measures. Most cover credit risk in the banking book, and the current standard horizon is two to three years (p. 5).\n2. Beware of macro stress tests as early warning devices (p. 6–9). ”To our\nknowledge, no macro stress test carried out ahead of the crisis identified the build-up of vulnerabilities” (p. 6). Two reasons. First, the models are partial equilibrium with weak feedbacks and are mis-specified, so the scenarios have to be ”unreasonably” large to produce distress (p. 6–7). Second, the context: financial crises tend to begin at the peak of the financial cycle, when indicators look healthiest (p. 7–9).\n3. They can greatly help in crisis management and resolution (p. 9). Once a crisis has erupted, vulnerabilities are visible, initial conditions are weak and prudence has replaced hubris, so ”it is not necessary to shake the system so hard to reveal weaknesses” (p. 9).\n4. Their additional benefits should not be underestimated (p. 9–10). They force economists, risk managers, loan officers and supervisors onto a common language, expose model outliers and improve data.\n5. If you do them, do them right (p. 10–12). Three elements of good practice: the will to really stress the system; buy-in by stakeholders; and a clear follow-up plan (p. 10). Use conservative loss estimates and severe scenarios, not overly constrained by history (p. 10). Consider leading indicators such as credit-to-GDP and property-price gaps and the debt-service ratio to decide when to run harder tests (p. 11). Pay more attention to common exposures than to bilateral interlinkages (p. 11–12).\n\nFour points that matter for your thesis\n• No error bands. ”Stress tests generally report no error bands around their forecasts but only point estimates” (p. 6, fn 7). The point-estimate impression is false precision, the same worry that Pindyck raises about IAMs in Layer 2.\n• Non-linearity. Most models are estimated ”as if the true world behaved in a log-linear fashion, when in fact it does not” (p. 6). In a severe case this understates losses.\n• Debt-service burden. In the annex (p. 13), ”as the costs of debt service relative to income increase, bank losses become more sensitive to a deteriorating macroeconomic environment.” Borrower leverage amplifies shocks.\n• A warning for Layer 4. The authors say ”too much attention is being paid to bilateral interlinkages and network analysis” and that a crisis ”acts like a tsunami that sweeps"},{"pdf":88,"printed":73,"ch":4,"text":"away all before it, not like a force that knocks down one domino after another” (p. 11– 12). That is a direct challenge to the contagion-network papers in Layer 4, so expect to argue with it.\n\nQuotables\nTable 4.3. Borio, Drehmann and Tsatsaronis (2014): Quotables\nQuote (short, verbatim)\n\nPage\n\nWhere it could be used\n\n”macro stress tests are ill-suited as early warning 3 (abstract) Stating the limits of any stress test, devices” including yours ”stress tests turned out to be part of the problem. 6 They lulled policymakers and market participants into a false sense of security.”\n\nArguing for scepticism towards\nreassuring results\n\n”No matter how hard one shook the box, little\nwould drop out.”\n\nExplaining why models with weak\namplification need huge shocks\n\n”The very essence of financial instability is that\nnormal-size shocks cause the system to break\ndown.”\n\n7–8\n\nContrasting with exogenous-shock\nstress tests\n\n”the system looks strongest precisely when it is\nmost vulnerable”\n\nWarning that healthy current ratios\ndo not prove resilience\n\n”the severity of the scenarios could be increased\nbased on the signals from reduced-form leading\nindicators of financial distress”\n\nJustifying a severe-case design when\nwarning indicators flash\n\n”A financial crisis acts like a tsunami that sweeps 12\naway all before it, not like a force that knocks\ndown one domino after another”\n\nDebating network-contagion models\n(Layer 4)\n\n”No stress test can succeed unless there is a\nstrong will to stress the system hard, to distrust\nrosy results and to be sceptical about seemingly\nstrong initial conditions.”\n\nClosing statement on method\n\nWhat it means for your thesis\n• How to describe your own stress test honestly. Say it is a conditional scenario analysis in the four-element format (exposures, scenario, model, outcome), say what is exogenous, and say what is left out (feedbacks, liquidity, second-round effects). This pre-empts the objections this paper makes.\n• Timing and horizon. The standard is two to three years (p. 5). Climate transition plays out over decades, so you need to state how you reconcile that horizon with the one used in your results (my observation; the paper says nothing about climate).\n• Capital targets as absolute amounts. The authors, citing Greenlaw et al., suggest setting capital targets in absolute amounts rather than as ratios to assets or risk-weighted assets, to limit an unintended credit crunch (p. 10). If your thesis ends with a capital shortfall, consider reporting it in rupees as well as in ratio points.\n• India. The paper does not mention India. It does say exchange rate risk may be more important for emerging markets (p. 4, fn 2)."},{"pdf":89,"printed":74,"ch":4,"text":"Can you skip the original? For the main argument, yes. It is only 13 pages and written in plain language, so I would still read Proposition 2 (p. 6–9) and Section 7 (p. 10–12) yourself because they set out the limitations you will need to cite. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.\n\n2. Basel Committee, The Basel Framework (consolidated standards,\ngenerated 15 October 2026)\nSource: Basel Committee on Banking Supervision, The Basel Framework, the full consolidated set of standards (your file ”basel-framework-full.pdf”, 1,429 pages). Page references below are the PDF page numbers (”N / 1429” in the footer), because the document is a web export with no printed page numbers. It is a living text, so cite it with the retrieval date. What I read. The contents; the minimum capital chapter (RBC20); the capital conservation and countercyclical buffers (RBC30); the IRB overview and risk-weight chapter openings (CRE30, CRE31); and every one of the 117 passages mentioning climate, which sit in FAQ boxes spread through the credit-risk chapters. I did not read the market-risk, operationalrisk, liquidity, large-exposure, securitisation or counterparty chapters, nor the definition of capital (CAP) in detail. Several formulas in the PDF are images, so I describe them in words and leave the algebra to Section 4 (Hull) and Section 5 (Bluhm).\n\nWhy this source matters most for your thesis\nThe Basel Framework is where the global rules for bank capital live. Its credit-risk chapters now carry a set of official FAQs on climate-related financial risks (the CRE20 change log says they were added on 8 December 2022 and updated with FAQs published on 10 June 2025). They tell banks, in the Committee's own words, that climate risk must be handled inside the existing credit-risk rules rather than through a new capital charge. That is the regulatory frame for a thesis on transition risk and capital adequacy.\n\nVocabulary\nTable 4.4. Basel Committee, The Basel Framework (consolidated standards: Vocabulary Term\n\nMeaning\n\nPillar 1\n\nMinimum capital for credit, market and operational risk.\n\nCET1 / Tier 1 / Total\ncapital\n\nCore equity-type capital; Tier 1 including additional Tier 1 instruments;\ntotal including Tier 2.\n\nConservation buffer\n\nExtra CET1 above the minimum; breach restricts dividends and bonuses,\nnot operations (RBC30, p. 157).\n\nCountercyclical buffer\n\nExtra CET1, 0 to 2.5% of RWA, set by national authorities in credit booms\n(RBC30, p. 160).\n\nStandardised approach\n(SA)\n\nRisk weights set by the regulator, mostly by external rating or exposure\ntype (CRE20 to CRE22).\n\nIRB approach\n\nBank estimates PD, LGD, EAD and maturity; a regulatory formula turns\nthem into capital (CRE30 to CRE36)."},{"pdf":90,"printed":75,"ch":4,"text":"Term\n\nMeaning\n\nSlotting\n\nSupervisory categories for specialised lending such as project finance\n(CRE33).\n\nExpected vs unexpected\nloss\n\nExpected loss is covered by provisions; capital covers unexpected loss\n(CRE30, p. 251).\n\n2.1 Minimum capital and buffers (global)\nTable 4.5. Basel Committee, The Basel Framework (consolidated standards: 2.1 Minimum capital and buffers (global) Requirement\n\nBasel minimum\n\nSource\n\nCET1\n\n4.5% of RWA\n\nRBC20.1, p. 139\n\nTier 1\n\nRBC20.1\n\nTotal capital\n\nRBC20.1\n\nConservation buffer (CET1)\n\n2.5%\n\nRBC30.2, p. 157; footnote to RBC20.1\n\nCountercyclical buffer\n\n0 to 2.5%\n\nRBC30.9, p. 160\n\nCompare with India (Section 3): RBI sets total capital at 9% and CET1 at 5.5%, so the Indian stack is 1 percentage point higher at each of CET1, Tier 1 and total capital. Quotable (RBC20.1, p. 139): ”Banks must meet the following requirements at all times: Common Equity Tier 1 must be at least 4.5% of risk-weighted assets (RWA). Tier 1 capital must be at least 6% of RWA. Total capital must be at least 8.0% of RWA.” Quotable (RBC30.2, p. 157): The conservation buffer of 2.5% is ”established above the regulatory minimum capital requirement. Capital distribution constraints will be imposed on a bank when capital levels fall within this range.” The constraints ”only relate to distributions, not the operation of the bank.” Quotable (RBC30.9, p. 160): National authorities facing ”a period of excess credit growth ... leading to the build up of system-wide risk” may require a countercyclical buffer that ”will vary between zero and 2.5% of risk weighted assets.”\n\n2.2 The two ways to compute credit risk RWAs\nStandardised. The regulator fixes the risk weight by exposure class and, for corporates and banks, by external rating. The bank's own judgement matters little, except through a duty of due diligence (below). Internal ratings-based. The bank estimates a probability of default (PD), loss given default (LGD), exposure at default (EAD) and, for corporates, effective maturity, and a prescribed formula converts them into a capital requirement. The IRB design splits loss in two: ”The risk-weight functions ... produce capital requirements for the UL portion. Expected losses are treated separately, as outlined in CRE35” (CRE30.2, p. 251). Defaulted exposures are handled separately: capital is the greater of zero and LGD minus the bank's best estimate of expected loss (CRE31.3, p. 264)."},{"pdf":91,"printed":76,"ch":4,"text":"Relevance to India. The RBI circular in Section 3 sets out the standardised approach in detail; I have not checked which Indian banks have supervisory approval for IRB (verify this before you rely on either route). Either way, the IRB climate FAQs are your guide to best practice and to how a PD-based stress test would work, not to current Indian rules.\n\n2.3 The climate-related FAQs: a guided map\nThe FAQs are short and all point the same way. I list them by the part of the credit-risk process they touch. Table 4.6. Basel Committee, The Basel Framework (consolidated standards: 2.3 The climate-related FAQs: a guided map PDF page\n\nWhere\n\nWhat the FAQ says\n\nSA due diligence (CRE20)\n\nBanks should integrate climate risk ”either in their own\ncredit risk assessment or when performing due diligence\non external ratings.”\n\n181\n\nSA exposures to banks\n(CRE21)\n\nBanks should consider material climate risk across ”the\nentire credit life cycle, including client due diligence as\npart of the onboarding process and ongoing monitoring.”\n\n188\n\nSA real estate (CRE20)\n\nBanks should check ”whether the current market value\nincorporates the potential changes in the value of\nproperties emerging from climate-related financial risks”\n(weather hazards, climate-policy standards, shifts in\nconsumption from transition policies).\n\n206\n\nSA risk weights, supervisors\n(CRE20)\n\nWhen judging if risk weights are too low, supervisors\n”should also consider climate-related financial risks.”\n\n204\n\nSpecialised lending slotting\n(CRE33)\n\nBanks should assess how climate risks affect the slotting\ncategory, for example transition risk through ”stability of\nlegal and regulatory environment (risk of change in law)”\nand physical risk through force majeure.\n\n293\n\nIRB rating assignment\n(CRE36)\n\nBanks ”should take into consideration material and\nrelevant information on the impact of climate-related\nfinancial risks on the borrower's financial condition”;\nwhere information is lacking, ”consider if it would be\nappropriate to take a more conservative approach”;\n”expert judgment” is allowed.\n\n316\n\nIRB rating criteria and horizon Ratings should use a horizon longer than one year, and (CRE36) the range of events considered ”should include climate-related financial risks, both physical and transition risks, if these materialise as credit risks.”\n\n317\n\nIRB stress testing (CRE36)\n\nBanks should ”iteratively and progressively consider\nclimate-related financial risks that affect the range of\npossible future economic conditions in their stress testing\nprocesses.”\n\n322\n\nIRB parameter estimation\n(CRE36)\n\nAdd ”a margin of conservatism due to data deficiencies,\nsuch as poor data quality or scarce climate-related data.”\n\n326"},{"pdf":92,"printed":77,"ch":4,"text":"Where\n\nWhat the FAQ says\n\nMapping internal grades to\nexternal ratings (CRE36)\n\nBanks should check whether the external agency's scale\n”reflects material climate-related financial risks” and\nadjust if it does not.\n\nPDF\npage\n330\n\nQuotable (rating criteria, p. 317): ”The range of economic conditions or unexpected events that should be considered when making the assessment of a borrower's ability to perform should include climate-related financial risks, both physical and transition risks, if these materialise as credit risks.” Quotable (stress testing, p. 322): ”Climate-related financial risks have the potential to impact banks' credit exposures and banks' assessment of credit risk, asset impairment and expected credit losses. Banks should iteratively and progressively consider climate-related financial risks that affect the range of possible future economic conditions in their stress testing processes.” Quotable (data limits, p. 326): Challenges in estimating PD, LGD and EAD ”include the range of impact uncertainties, limitations in the availability and relevance of historical data describing the relationship of climate risk drivers to traditional financial risks, and questions around the time horizon.” Banks ”should add a margin of conservatism due to data deficiencies.” Quotable (real estate collateral, p. 206): Banks should determine ”whether the current market value incorporates the potential changes in the value of properties emerging from climaterelated financial risks.”\n\n2.4 What this does and does not do\n• It is guidance on process, not a new capital number. No FAQ changes a risk\nweight or adds a climate charge. The Committee's position is that climate risk is a driver of credit risk, so it should enter PD, LGD, ratings and collateral values. This is the ”driver, not a separate risk type” view you will meet again in the NGFS material in Layer 4.\n• It admits the data problem. The repeated phrases ”iteratively and progressively”, ”conservative” and ”expert judgment” signal that regulators accept immature methods. Your thesis can cite this as the regulatory licence for a stylised scenario approach.\n• It names transition risk concretely (changes in law, climate-policy standards, shifts in consumption), which gives you a regulatory definition to align your ”transition risk” variable with.\n• Separate Basel documents are not in this file. The Committee's 2022 principles for the management and supervision of climate-related financial risks, and its disclosure framework, are different publications. I have not read them.\n\n2.5 How this feeds the thesis\n1. Legitimacy: the global standard-setter says climate risk belongs in credit risk assessment and stress testing, so a capital-adequacy stress test is a recognised approach.\n2. Mechanism: transition risk reaches capital through ratings, PD and LGD, collateral"},{"pdf":93,"printed":78,"ch":4,"text":"values and the stress-test scenario. Those are your model's channels.\n3. Benchmark: compare the Basel 4.5/6/8% stack with the RBI 5.5/7/9% stack when you set the adequacy threshold.\n4. Limitation to cite: the Framework itself says historical data are weak for climate risk, which supports using scenario analysis.\n\n3. RBI, Master Circular: Basel III Capital Regulations (1 April 2025)\nSource: Reserve Bank of India, Master Circular on Basel III Capital Regulations, dated 1 April 2025 (your file ”Basel III Capital Regulations RBI (2025).PDF”). Page references are the printed page numbers of the circular. Important flag: this document has been replaced. Every page of your copy carries a ”Withdrawn” watermark. RBI's Master Directions page lists Reserve Bank of India (Commercial Banks – Prudential Norms on Capital Adequacy) Directions, 2025, issued 28 November 2025 (notification RBI/DOR/2025-26/151, per a secondary summary), and says earlier Master Circulars ”will stand withdrawn with the issue of the Master Direction on the subject.” I could not open the new Directions themselves, so I have not checked whether the numbers below changed. For the thesis: use this circular for how the framework worked up to November 2025, and cite the 2025 Directions for anything stated as current. Before you quote a figure in the thesis, check it against the new text. What I read. The table of contents, Part A sections 4 and 5 (capital composition, minima, and credit-risk weights), the leverage ratio and countercyclical buffer sections, and the ICAAP stress-testing paragraph. I did not read the market-risk, operational-risk, counterparty-risk or securitisation sections, or the annexes. The circular contains no climate or ESG content (I searched the full text). That is itself a useful fact: climate risk entered Indian bank capital rules, if at all, only through the general channels below.\n\nVocabulary\nTable 4.7. RBI, Master Circular: Basel III Capital Regulations (1 April 2025): Vocabulary Term\n\nMeaning\n\nCRAR\n\nCapital to Risk-weighted Assets Ratio, RBI's name for the total capital ratio (p. 11).\n\nCET1 / AT1 /\nTier 2\n\nCommon Equity Tier 1; Additional Tier 1; Tier 2. Tier 1 is ”going-concern” capital and Tier 2 ”gone-concern” capital (p. 11, footnote 5).\n\nCCB\n\nCapital Conservation Buffer, 2.5% of RWAs, held in CET1 (p. 12).\n\nCCCB\n\nCountercyclical Capital Buffer, 0 to 2.5% of RWAs, in CET1 (p. 181).\n\nICAAP / SREP Bank's own internal capital assessment; RBI's supervisory review of it (Pillar 2). Leverage ratio\n\nCapital divided by total (non-risk-weighted) exposure (p. 167).\n\n3.1 The minimum capital stack (p. 11–12)\nThe ratio is computed as eligible capital over the sum of credit, market and operational risk RWAs. The circular states it in three lines: CET1 ratio, Tier 1 ratio and Total Capital (CRAR),"},{"pdf":94,"printed":79,"ch":4,"text":"each with the same denominator ”Credit Risk RWA + Market Risk RWA + Operational Risk RWA” (p. 11). Table 4.8. RBI, Master Circular: Basel III Capital Regulations (1 April 2025): 3.1 The minimum capital stack (p. 11–12) Layer\n\n% of RWAs\n\nSource\n\nMinimum CET1\n\n5.5\n\np. 12 table, row (i)\n\nCapital conservation buffer (CET1)\n\n2.5\n\nrow (ii)\n\nCET1 plus CCB\n\n8.0\n\nrow (iii)\n\nAdditional Tier 1\n\n1.5\n\nrow (iv)\n\nMinimum Tier 1\n\n7.0\n\nrow (v)\n\nTier 2\n\n2.0\n\nrow (vi)\n\nMinimum total capital\n\n9.0\n\nrow (vii)\n\nMinimum total capital plus CCB\n\n11.5\n\nrow (viii)\n\nWhy India's 9% differs from the Basel 8%. The Basel Committee's minimum is 8%. RBI requires 9% (4.2.2(i), p. 11: ”As a matter of prudence ... a minimum total capital (MTC) of 9% of total risk weighted assets”). That is one reason Indian banks usually report CRAR well above the Basel floor, which matters when you interpret ”capital adequacy” in your dependent variable. Quotable (p. 11): ”Banks shall maintain a minimum Pillar 1 Capital to Risk-weighted Assets Ratio (CRAR) of 9% on an on-going basis (other than capital conservation buffer and countercyclical capital buffer etc.).” Quotable (p. 11): The Reserve Bank ”will consider prescribing a higher level of minimum capital ratio for each bank under the Pillar 2 framework on the basis of their respective risk profiles and their risk management systems.” Pillar 2 is the legal hook for a bank-specific climate add-on.\n\n3.2 Risk weights: where credit risk and climate risk would meet\nUnder the standardised approach the risk weight depends on the counterparty or the product. These are the weights that matter for a public sector bank's loan book. Table 4.9. RBI, Master Circular: Basel III Capital Regulations (1 April 2025): 3.2 Risk weights: where credit risk and climate risk would meet Exposure\n\nRisk weight\n\nSource\n\nCentral government claims\n\n5.2.1 (page not\nchecked)\n\nCorporates and NBFCs, long term, rated\nAAA / AA / A / BBB / BB and below\n\n20 / 30 / 50 / 100 / 150%\n\nTable 5 Part A, p.\n\nUnrated corporates\n\n100%; 150% if aggregate\nTable 5, notes (iii),\nbanking-system exposure exceeds p. 40\n₹200 crore\n\nPreviously rated, now unrated, exposure\nabove ₹100 crore\n\n150%\n\nTable 5, note (ii), p."},{"pdf":95,"printed":80,"ch":4,"text":"Exposure\n\nRisk weight\n\nSource\n\nIndividual housing loans up to ₹30 lakh,\nLTV up to 80%\n\n35%\n\nTable 7, p. 43\n\nCommercial real estate\n\n100% (residential CRE 75%)\n\nTable 7, p. 43;\n5.11.2, p. 44\n\nConsumer credit (excluding housing,\neducation, vehicle, gold)\n\n125%\n\n5.13.3, p. 46\n\nCapital market exposures\n\n125% or rating-based, whichever\nis higher\n\n5.13.4, p. 46\n\nTwo points follow for the thesis. First, most Indian corporate borrowers are unrated, so they sit at a flat 100%. A climate-exposed firm and a climate-safe firm with the same rating carry the same weight, which means the standardised approach has no direct channel for climate risk. Second, risk weights move only when a rating changes or a loan turns non-performing. Transition risk reaches bank capital through those two events. Quotable (5.8.1, p. 39–40): Claims on corporates ”will be risk weighted as per the ratings assigned by the rating agencies registered with SEBI and accredited by the Reserve Bank of India.” This ties capital to rating agencies, whose climate treatment is a separate question.\n\n3.3 Non-performing assets and capital (5.12, p. 45)\nThe unsecured portion of an NPA, net of specific provisions, is weighted by how much has been provided:\n• 150% if specific provisions are below 20% of the outstanding amount,\n• 100% if at least 20%,\n• 50% if at least 50% (5.12.1).\nSo a deteriorating loan raises RWAs at the same moment provisions reduce capital. This double hit is the mechanism by which a transition shock to borrowers shows up in CRAR. Land, buildings, plant and machinery are not counted as secured collateral for this purpose except in a narrow case with 15% provisions (5.12.3 to 5.12.4, p. 45); that matters for carbonintensive industrial borrowers whose collateral is heavy plant.\n\n3.4 Buffers, leverage and stress testing\n• Countercyclical buffer (17.2, p. 181). It may range from 0 to 2.5% of RWAs, in CET1. The main indicator is the credit-to-GDP gap, ”used in conjunction with GNPA growth” (17.2.3). The buffer starts at a gap of 3 percentage points and reaches its maximum at 15 (17.2.4). Decisions are normally pre-announced four quarters ahead (17.2.2). This is a worked example of the financial-cycle logic in Borio et al. (Section 1).\n• Leverage ratio (16.2.2, p. 167). Minimum 4% for domestic systemically important banks (D-SIBs), and 3.5% for others.\n• ICAAP stress tests (12.10, p. 133). Banks must ”conduct relevant stress tests periodically ... to evaluate the potential vulnerability of the bank to some unlikely but"},{"pdf":96,"printed":81,"ch":4,"text":"plausible events or movements in the market conditions.” This is the clause under which a bank, or RBI, can require a climate scenario. It does not mention climate. Quotable (12.10, p. 133): Stress testing evaluates ”the potential vulnerability of the bank to some unlikely but plausible events” and can give management ”a better understanding of the bank's likely exposure in extreme circumstances.”\n\n3.5 How this feeds the thesis\n• It supplies your definition of capital adequacy: CRAR with CET1 and buffer components, and the 9% and 11.5% thresholds, are the benchmarks against which a simulated capital loss is judged.\n• It supplies the transmission mechanism: rating downgrade or NPA classification, higher risk weight, higher provisions, lower capital.\n• It supplies the gap: the framework has no climate-specific term, so any climate effect has to be modelled through PD, LGD or ratings.\n• Citation caution: the withdrawn circular is fine as a historical source, but mark it as superseded and cite the 2025 Directions for current rules.\n\n4. Hull, Risk Management and Financial Institutions (4th ed., 2015)\nSource: Hull, J. C. (2015), Risk Management and Financial Institutions, 4th edition, Wiley (your file ”Hull, Risk Management and Financial Institutions.pdf”, 743 pages). Page references are the book's printed pages. This is a textbook, so use it to understand mechanisms and cite the primary source (Basel, RBI) for rules. What I read. Chapter 2 (Section 2.2, a toy bank and its capital), Chapter 15 (Basel I and Basel II, including the IRB formula and the three pillars), Chapter 16 (Section 16.2, Basel III) and Chapter 22 (scenario analysis and stress testing). I did not read the market-risk, VaR, derivatives, liquidity, insurance or operational-risk chapters. Two cautions: (1) the 4th edition predates the 2017 finalisation of Basel III, so its Basel III numbers match the 2010 rules, not today's; (2) the book contains no climate content.\n\nWhy read it\nHull gives you three things the regulatory texts do not: a worked example of why a bank needs capital, the logic behind the IRB capital formula, and a practical account of how stress tests are built and misused.\n\nVocabulary\nTable 4.10. Hull, Risk Management and Financial Institutions (4th ed., 2015): Vocabulary Term\n\nMeaning\n\nROE\n\nReturn on equity: profit divided by equity capital (p. 29)."},{"pdf":97,"printed":82,"ch":4,"text":"Term\n\nMeaning\n\nWCDR\n\nWorst-case default rate: the default rate the bank is 99.9% sure will not be\nexceeded next year (p. 341).\n\nGaussian copula\n\nA model that links obligors' defaults through one common factor with\ncorrelation ρ (p. 340–341).\n\nFoundation vs\nAdvanced IRB\n\nFoundation: bank supplies PD only. Advanced: bank also supplies LGD, EAD\nand maturity (p. 343).\n\nReverse stress test\n\nSearch for the scenario that causes failure (p. 468).\n\n4.1 Why banks need capital: the DLC example (Chapter 2, p. 28–30)\nHull builds a hypothetical bank, Deposits and Loans Corporation (DLC), with $100 million of assets: $80m loans, $15m cash and securities, $5m fixed assets; funded by $90m deposits, $5m subordinated debt and $5m equity (Table 2.2, p. 28). Loan losses in a normal year are 0.8% of assets (Table 2.3, p. 29). The stress test: loan losses jump by 3.2% of assets to 4% in a severe recession. Pre-tax operating income moves from +0.6% to −2.6% of assets; after 30% tax the loss is about 1.8% of assets. Equity falls from 5% to 3.2% and survives. If equity had been only 1% (Table 2.4, p. 30), one such year would wipe it out. Quotable (p. 29): ”How much equity capital does DLC need? This question can be answered by hypothesizing an extremely adverse scenario and considering whether the bank would survive.”\n\nUse in the thesis. This is the simplest possible version of what your model does: shock loan losses, push them through the income statement, and see what is left of capital. You can cite it to justify the logic of a loss-shock design before moving to the richer methods in Sections 5 and 6. Quotable (p. 30): ”Equity provides the best protection against adverse events”; subordinated debt ”does not provide as good a cushion for the bank as equity because it does not prevent the bank's insolvency.”\n\n4.2 Risk-weighted assets and the Basel I to Basel II step (Chapter 15)\nBasel I gave every corporate loan a 100% weight regardless of credit quality (p. 336: ”A loan to a corporation with a AAA credit rating is treated in the same way as one to a corporation with a B credit rating”). Basel II made weights depend on ratings (standardised approach) or on the bank's own estimates (IRB). Table 4.11. Hull, Risk Management and Financial Institutions (4th ed., 2015): 4.2 Risk-weighted assets and the Basel I to Basel II step (Chapter 15) Exposure (Basel II standardised, Table 15.4, p. 338)\n\nAAA to\nAA−\n\nA+ to\n\nBBB+\nBBB−\n\nCorporations\n\n20%\n\n50%\n\n100%\n\nBB+ to\n\nBelow\n\nUnrated\n\n100 to\n150%\n\n150%\n\n100%"},{"pdf":98,"printed":83,"ch":4,"text":"Other Basel II weights quoted by Hull: retail 75%, residential mortgage 35%, commercial real estate 100% (p. 338). Worked example (Example 15.5, p. 339): $100m of A-rated corporate loans, $10m AAA government bonds and $50m of residential mortgages give RWA of 0.5×100 + 0×10 + 0.35×50 = $67.5m, against $125m under Basel I. Total capital must be 8% of RWA (equation 15.5, p. 337); a direct capital charge is multiplied by 12.5 to convert it to an RWA equivalent. The three pillars (p. 336 to 337, 346 to 347). Pillar 1 minimum capital; Pillar 2 supervisory review (banks must assess their own capital adequacy and supervisors may require more); Pillar 3 market discipline through disclosure. Pillar 2 expects banks to run credit-risk stress tests (p. 347).\n\n4.3 The IRB capital formula, step by step (p. 340 to 343)\nThis is the most useful technical passage for a capital-adequacy thesis.\n1. Idea. Capital is set so the bank survives a bad year with 99.9% confidence. ”The capital required is therefore the value at risk minus the expected loss” (p. 340), because expected losses are already priced into loan interest.\n2. Worst-case default rate, from a one-factor Gaussian copula with PD and correlation ρ (equation 15.6, p. 341): N −1 (P D) + ρ N −1 (0.999) W CDR = N 1−ρ\n\n3. Capital for the portfolio is the sum over loans of (equation 15.7, p. 341):\n\nEAD × LGD × (W CDR − P D)\n4. Corporate exposures also use a correlation that falls as PD rises (equation 15.8, p. 342) and a maturity adjustment MA (equation 15.10, p. 343), so capital = EAD × LGD × (WCDR − PD) × MA (15.9, p. 342) and RWA = 12.5 × that amount (p. 343). The table below (Table 15.6, p. 343) shows how WCDR responds to PD for corporate exposures. Read it as a sensitivity: a rise in PD of the sort a transition shock could cause raises the capital charge sharply. Table 4.12. Hull, Risk Management and Financial Institutions (4th ed., 2015): 4.3 The IRB capital formula, step by step (p. 340 to 343)\n\n0.1%\n\n0.5%\n\n1.5%\n\n2.0%\n\nWCDR\n\n3.4%\n\n9.8%\n\n14.0%\n\n16.9%\n\n19.0%\n\nUnder Foundation IRB, LGD is fixed at 45% for senior and 75% for subordinated claims, and maturity at 2.5 years in most cases (p. 343). Note: these parameters are from the 2015 edition; the Basel Framework (Section 2) has the current ones."},{"pdf":99,"printed":84,"ch":4,"text":"Quotable (p. 340): ”Regulators base the capital requirement on the value at risk calculated using a one-year time horizon and a 99.9% confidence level. They recognize that expected losses are usually covered by the way a financial institution prices its products.”\n\nUse in the thesis. If your model raises PD or LGD for carbon-intensive borrowers, equation\n15.9 and Table 15.6 show how the regulatory capital requirement would rise. That is a clean, citable transmission from a climate-induced change in credit quality to the denominator of CRAR.\n\n4.4 Basel III in Hull's account (Section 16.2, p. 357 to 361)\n• Total capital = Tier 1 equity + additional Tier 1 + Tier 2; ”There is no Tier 3 capital” (p. 357).\n• Minimums: Tier 1 equity 4.5%, total Tier 1 6%, total 8% of RWA (p. 358). Compare Basel I: 2% and 4% (p. 358).\n• Conservation buffer: a further 2.5% in core equity; total requirement in normal times is 7% for equity, 8.5% for Tier 1 and 10.5% for total capital (p. 359).\n• Dividend limits as equity falls into the buffer (Table 16.2, p. 359): for example 80% of earnings must be retained when equity is between 5.125% and 5.75%.\n• Countercyclical buffer 0 to 2.5%, set by national authorities (p. 359).\n• Leverage ratio: 3% minimum in the 2015 text, based on Tier 1 over unweighted exposure (p. 360). Hull explains its rationale: regulators ”thought that banks had too much discretion in the way risk-weighted assets were calculated.” These match the Basel Framework figures in Section 2 and give you the narrative behind them.\n\n4.5 Scenario analysis and stress testing (Chapter 22, p. 463 to 475)\nHull's chapter reads like a design manual for a stress test.\nQuotable (p. 463): ”Stress testing involves evaluating the impact of extreme, but plausible, scenarios that are not considered by value at risk (VaR) or expected shortfall (ES) models.”\n\nWays to generate scenarios (p. 464 to 466). Stress one variable; replay a historical episode of several variables moving together; magnify history; or have senior management brainstorm ”What can go wrong?” He prefers management-built scenarios, with a committee of three to five people with different backgrounds (p. 465). Climate scenarios are management-built in this sense, since they are not found in the historical record. Core and peripheral variables (p. 466). When only a few variables are stressed, the others should not just be held constant; forecast them conditional on the core shocks, using stressed correlations. Knock-on effects (p. 466 to 468). A scenario is incomplete if it ignores what happens when many institutions respond the same way. The LTCM case shows a stress test that was correct about the first-round shock and wrong about the second. Compare Borio et al. on contagion in Section 1."},{"pdf":100,"printed":85,"ch":4,"text":"Quotable (p. 466): Scenarios ”should include not only the immediate effect on the financial institution's portfolio of shock to market variables, but also any 'knock-on' effect resulting from many different financial institutions being affected by the shock in the same way and responding in the same way.”\n\nReverse stress testing (p. 468). Search for scenarios that cause failure. This is one way to ask how large a transition shock would have to be to push a bank below its CRAR minimum. Using the results (p. 473). ”The biggest problem in stress testing is using the results effectively”; managers tend to say ”there are always one or two scenarios that will sink us.” Hull urges the better question: ”Are the risks associated with these scenarios acceptable?” Hull also describes assigning probabilities to stress scenarios so they can be combined with VaR (p. 474), a device that may help you rank scenarios.\n\n4.6 How this feeds the thesis\n• Mechanism: DLC (p. 28 to 30) and the IRB formula (p. 340 to 343) show how a loss shock and a PD shock reach capital.\n• Method: Chapter 22 gives vocabulary and design principles for your scenario analysis, and supports an honest limitations section.\n• Limit: a 2015 textbook; use it for mechanisms and cite RBI or Basel for current rules.\n\n5. Bluhm, Overbeck and Wagner, An Introduction to Credit Risk\nModeling (2nd ed., 2010)\nSource: Bluhm, C., Overbeck, L. and Wagner, C. (2010), An Introduction to Credit Risk Modeling, 2nd edition, Chapman & Hall/CRC (your file ”Bluhm, Overbeck & Wagner, An Introduction to Credit Risk Modeling (Book).pdf”, 385 pages). Page references are the book's printed pages. What I read. From Chapter 1: expected loss, LGD, the PD-LGD-EAD dependence remark, unexpected loss, economic capital and the Basel section (pages 1 to 28 and 45 to 48). From Chapter 2: the CreditPortfolioView model (pages 71 to 77) and the start of the one-factor model (pages 80 to 87). I did not read the CreditRisk+ chapter, the asset value model chapter in full, risk contributions, the structured-credit chapters or the appendices. The book contains no climate content.\n\nWhy read it\nThis is the technical backbone for any model that moves a bank's loss and capital with the economy. Two ideas matter most for you: the split of loss into expected and unexpected parts, and the CreditPortfolioView framework, which makes default probabilities depend on macroeconomic variables. Replace ”macro variables” with ”climate-transition variables” and you have the structure of a climate-conditioned credit model.\n\nVocabulary"},{"pdf":101,"printed":86,"ch":4,"text":"Table 4.13. Bluhm, Overbeck and Wagner, An Introduction to Credit Risk M: Vocabulary Term\n\nMeaning\n\nPD / LGD / EAD\n\nProbability of default; fraction of exposure lost if default occurs; amount\nexposed at default (p. 3).\n\nExpected loss (EL)\n\nThe average loss; priced into the loan and covered by provisions (p. 3 to 4).\n\nUnexpected loss (UL)\n\nThe standard deviation of loss around the mean (p. 22).\n\nEconomic capital (EC) A high quantile of the loss distribution minus expected loss (p. 27). Loss distribution\n\nThe probability distribution of portfolio loss; its shape fixes EL, UL and EC\n(p. 29).\n\nMigration matrix\n\nProbabilities of moving between rating classes in a year, including default (p. 72).\n\nConditional vs\nunconditional\n\nConditional: given the state of the economy; unconditional: averaged over\nstates (p. 72).\n\nOne-factor model\n\nAll obligors share one systematic factor Y with asset correlation ρ (p. 80).\n\n5.1 Expected loss (Section 1.1, p. 2 to 4)\nEach loan has a loss variable: exposure times loss fraction times a default indicator. L̃ = EAD × LGD × 1D ,\n\nP (D) = P D\n\nIts expectation is the expected loss. With EAD and LGD independent of default (Proposition 1.1.3, p. 4): EL = EAD × LGD × P D The authors treat EL as an insurance premium charged to borrowers and set aside as a reserve (p. 2). They add a caution: for banks under US accounting rules, loss reserves ”can not be used in the afore-mentioned sense of an expected loss reserve”, so the reserve is partly a theoretical concept (Remark 1.1.1, p. 2). The same applies in practice to provisioning rules in other countries. Connection to India (Section 6 will cover NPAs). Specific provisions and the PD-LGDEAD triple are the technical language behind gross and net NPA ratios.\n\n5.2 The independence assumption is wrong in a recession (Section 1.1.4, p. 21 to 22) The simple formula for EL assumes PD, LGD and EAD are independent. The authors argue this fails exactly in bad times:\n• In a recession defaults rise, banks sell more collateral, collateral prices fall, recoveries fall, and so LGD rises with PD.\n• Firms in distress draw on open credit lines, so EAD rises with PD too."},{"pdf":102,"printed":87,"ch":4,"text":"Quotable (p. 21): ”defaults and recoveries to some extent are influenced by the same underlying systematic risk drivers so that they can not be independent.”\n\nUse in the thesis. A climate shock that cuts collateral values (stranded assets, flood-exposed property) is the same mechanism. It supports modelling a shock to LGD together with PD, rather than PD alone. The Basel real-estate FAQ on climate and property values (Section 2, p. 206) says the same in regulatory language.\n\n5.3 Unexpected loss and economic capital (Sections 1.2, p. 22 to 29)\n• Unexpected loss is the standard deviation of the loss variable (Definition 1.2.1, p. 22). For one loan with deterministic EAD (Proposition 1.2.2, p. 22 to 23):\n\nU L = EAD\n\nV ar[LGD] P D + E[LGD]2 P D(1 − P D)\n\n• Economic capital at confidence level α is the α-quantile of portfolio loss minus the portfolio's expected loss (Definition 1.2.8, equation 1.15, p. 27):\n\nECα = qα − ELP F\n• With α = 99.98% the capital covers losses in 9,998 out of 10,000 years; it will fail in the other 2 (p. 28).\n• EL charges do not depend on the rest of the portfolio, but EC charges do: a loan that adds concentration needs more capital than the same loan in a diversified book (p. 28). This is the formal case for concentration risk, which a heavily exposed power, steel or cement book creates. The regulatory IRB formula in Hull (Section 4.3) is this logic with α = 99.9% and a Gaussian one-factor model. Quotable (p. 28): ”the EL charges are portfolio independent, but EC charges are portfolio dependent.”\n\n5.4 The Basel section (Section 1.3, p. 45 to 48)\nThe authors give a short history. Basel I applied a flat 8% to risk-weighted assets, with weights of 0% for governments, 20% for OECD banks and 100% for corporates ”no matter if the borrowing firm is a more or less risky obligor” (p. 46). Basel II introduced risk-sensitive weights, and they restate the IRB formula (p. 47): RW A = 12.5 × EAD × LGD × K(P D) × M (P D, M AT ) where K(PD) is the 99.9% quantile of the limiting loss distribution minus PD, with correlation ρ(PD) falling as PD rises (p. 47 to 48). They note that Basel II ”do[es] not allow the use of internal credit risk models for the calculation of regulatory capital” (p. 47), only the prescribed formula. This matches Hull's account, and the Basel Framework (Section 2) is the current text."},{"pdf":103,"printed":88,"ch":4,"text":"5.5 The one-factor model: where correlation and the cycle enter (Section 2.5.1, p. 80 to 87) All obligors' asset returns share one systematic factor Y (the ”state of the economy”) plus an idiosyncratic part (equation 2.48, p. 80). Conditional on Y, the default probability of obligor i is (equation 2.49, p. 80):\n\nN −1 [pi ] − ρ Y\npi (Y ) = N\n1−ρ\nA bad economy (low Y) raises everyone's default probability at once. For a large portfolio the share of defaults converges to this expression (equation 2.54, p. 87), which is what the Basel IRB formula evaluates at the 99.9% worst-case Y. If you build a stylised loss model, this is the standard way to turn a single systematic shock into a portfolio default rate.\n\n5.6 CreditPortfolioView: macro variables drive defaults (Section 2.4.3, p. 71 to 77) This section is the most directly useful for your thesis. Quotable (p. 71 to 72): CreditPortfolioView (CPV) ”is a ratings-based portfolio model incorporating the dependence of default and migration probabilities on the economic cycle.”\n\nHow it works\n1. Start with an average (unconditional) rating migration matrix M (p. 72).\n2. Divide the economy into risk segments, such as industry groups, each reacting differently to the cycle (p. 73).\n3. For each segment, simulate a conditional default probability p_s. Define a risk index r_s = p_s / (the segment's average default probability) (equation 2.41, p. 73).\n4. Shift the migration matrix: m_ij(s) = α_ij (r_s − 1) + m_ij (equation 2.42, p. 73). If r_s < 1 the economy is expanding and downgrades fall; if r_s > 1 it is a recession and downgrades rise (p. 74 to 75).\n5. Build a loss distribution for each scenario, then aggregate (p. 75).\nWhere macro variables enter (CPV Macro, p. 76 to 77). Each segment has an index driven by macro variables, such as ”the unemployment rate, the growth rate of the Gross Domestic Product (GDP), interest or currency exchange rates” (p. 76):\n\nYs,t = ws,0 +\n\nws,k Xs,k,t + εs,t\n\nk=1\n\nThe macro variables follow autoregressive processes (equation 2.45; Wilson used AR(2)), and the conditional default probability is a logit transform of the index (equation 2.46, p. 77): ps,t =\n\n1 + exp(Ys,t )"},{"pdf":104,"printed":89,"ch":4,"text":"An alternative, CPV Direct, skips the macro regression and draws segment default probabilities from a gamma distribution, which is easier to calibrate (p. 75 to 77). The authors note CPV can ”incorporate stressed tails in the systematic risk model ... in order to study the impact of heavy recessions” (p. 75). Use in the thesis.\n• The structure (macro or policy variables → segment index → PD and migration → loss → capital) is the template for a transition-risk credit model. You would add a carbonprice or policy variable to X and let carbon-intensive sectors (power, steel, cement, oil and gas) be separate risk segments with their own coefficients.\n• The segment idea matches the sector-wise exposure data you will take from the RBI reports (Section 6).\n• The calibration difficulty the authors stress (many parameters, ”The calibration of CPV Macro is more complicated”, p. 75) is a limitation you can cite when you choose a simpler approach.\n\n5.7 How this feeds the thesis\n• Concepts: EL, UL, EC and the loss distribution, with precise definitions you can cite.\n• Mechanism: PD, LGD and EAD move together in a downturn (p. 21), so shock them jointly.\n• Model template: CreditPortfolioView (p. 71 to 77) and the one-factor model (p. 80 to 87).\n• Limit: a 2010 text, written before the 2017 Basel changes; use it for modelling, not for current rules.\n\n6. RBI, Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India (2007-08\nto 2024-25)\nSource: Reserve Bank of India, Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India, the annual statutory report submitted to the Central Government under Section 36(2) of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949 (cover text of each file). Your folder holds 18 PDFs, one per year, mostly under opaque file names. Page references below are printed page numbers of the edition named. What I read. From the 2024-25 edition (published December 2025): the capital adequacy, leverage, asset quality and sectoral credit passages and tables of Chapter IV, the climate passages in Chapters I and III, and one appendix table. From earlier editions I read only the passages cited: Basel II implementation (2007-08), Basel III phasing (2012-13), the NPA peak (2017-18 and 2018-19), and the climate and green-finance passages (2021-22, 2022-23, 2023-24). I did not read the other editions, the chapters on co-operative banks, NBFCs, payments or financial inclusion, or the appendix tables beyond the one cited. The report series is aggregate: it gives bank-group totals (PSBs, private banks and so on), not bank-by-bank data.\n\nThe files in your folder"},{"pdf":105,"printed":90,"ch":4,"text":"Table 4.14. RBI, Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India (2007-: The files in your folder Edition (cover text)\n\nYour file name starts with\n\nPages\n\n2007-08 (year ended June 2008)\n\n88980.pdf\n\n508\n\n2008-09\n\nRTP0809PRD_Full.pdf\n\n413\n\n2009-10\n\nRTP081110FL.pdf\n\n218\n\n2010-11\n\n0TPBI121111_FULL.pdf\n\n215\n\n2011-12\n\n0TPB021112FLS.pdf\n\n191\n\n2012-13\n\n0RTP21112013_F.pdf\n\n189\n\n2013-14 (published as Financial Stability Report, FSR29122014_FL.pdf\nDecember 2014, including the Trend and\nProgress chapters)\n\n2014-15 (partial: 40 pages only)\n\n0FLTP577BF4E...\n\n2015-16 (partial: 56 pages only)\n\n0FRTP16A120A...\n\n2016-17\n\n0RTP20161778B7...\n\n216\n\n2017-18\n\n0RTP2018_FE9E97...\n\n199\n\n2018-19\n\n0RTP241219FL760D...\n\n194\n\n2019-20 (cover reads ”year ended June 30, 2020” 0RTP2020_F3D078...\nbut the title page says 2019-20)\n\n230\n\n2020-21\n\n0RTP2020CF9C9E...\n\n248\n\n2021-22\n\n0RTP20212225730A...\n\n234\n\n2022-23\n\n0RTP27122023D93943...\n\n228\n\n2023-24\n\n0RTP261220247FFF1F...\n\n228\n\n2024-25\n\n0RTP291220258C89B9...\n\n220\n\nTwo flags. The 2014-15 and 2015-16 files are short, so they are extracts, not full reports. The 2019-20 cover wording is RBI's own mismatch, not a file error. The covers of the older editions say ”year ended June 30”, which I take to reflect RBI's earlier July to June reporting year (my inference); the 2020-21 and later covers say ”year ended March 31”.\n\nVocabulary\nTable 4.15. RBI, Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India (2007-: Vocabulary Term\n\nMeaning\n\nSCB, PSB, PVB,\nFB, SFB, RRB\n\nScheduled commercial banks; public sector, private, foreign, small finance and regional rural banks.\n\nGNPA / NNPA\nratio\n\nGross (net) non-performing assets as a share of gross (net) advances.\n\nPCR\n\nProvision coverage ratio: provisions held against NPAs (here not adjusted for\nwrite-offs).\n\nSlippage ratio\n\nNew NPAs in the year as a share of standard advances at the start of the year (p. 59, para IV.30).\n\nSMA\n\nSpecial mention accounts: loans showing early stress before they become NPAs.\n\nRegulated entity, RBI's term for banks and other supervised institutions."},{"pdf":106,"printed":91,"ch":4,"text":"Term\n\nMeaning\n\nRB-CRIS\n\nReserve Bank Climate Risk Information System: a planned climate data\nrepository.\n\n6.1 Where the banks stand: 2024-25 snapshot\nThe 2024-25 edition reports figures at end-March 2025 unless noted.\nTable 4.16. RBI, Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India (2007-: 6.1 Where the banks stand: 2024-25 snapshot Indicator\n\nPSBs\n\nAll SCBs\n\nSource\n\nCapital funds (₹ crore)\n\n13,55,864\n\n31,78,386\n\nTable IV.5, p. 58\n\nRisk-weighted assets (₹ crore)\n\n84,23,011\n\n1,83,05,026\n\nTable IV.5\n\nCRAR (%)\n\n16.1 (15.5 a year\nearlier)\n\n17.4 (16.9)\n\nTable IV.5\n\nTier 1 ratio (%)\n\n13.8\n\n15.5\n\nTable IV.5\n\nLeverage ratio (%)\n\n6.3\n\n8.0\n\nTable IV.7, p. 59\n\nGNPA ratio (%)\n\n2.6 (3.5 a year\nearlier)\n\n2.2 (2.7)\n\nTable IV.9, p. 60\n\nNet NPA ratio (%)\n\n0.5 (0.8)\n\n0.5 (0.6)\n\nTable IV.9\n\nProvision coverage ratio (%)\n\n78.5\n\n76.3\n\npara IV.22, p. 56\n\nGross NPAs written off in 2024-25\n(₹ crore)\n\n91,176\n\n1,57,563\n\nTable IV.9\n\nQuotable (IV.24, p. 57): ”In India, the minimum regulatory capital to risk-weighted assets ratio (CRAR) requirement for banks is set at 9.0 per cent [11.5 per cent inclusive of capital conservation buffer (CCB)] and Tier 1 capital ratio requirement at 7.0 per cent, both one percentage point above the Basel III norms.” Quotable (IV.29, p. 59): ”The GNPA ratio of SCBs declined further to a multi-decadal low of\n2.2 per cent at end-March 2025 from 2.7 per cent at end-March 2024.” The slippage ratio ”declined for the fifth consecutive year to 1.4 per cent” (IV.30, p. 59).\n\nBack-of-envelope headroom (my calculation, not RBI's). For PSBs, 11.5% of RWA is ₹9.69 lakh crore. Total capital of ₹13.56 lakh crore exceeds that by about ₹3.87 lakh crore, or 4.6 percentage points of RWA. For scale, the entire stock of PSB gross NPAs at end-March 2025 was ₹2.84 lakh crore (Table IV.9). This ignores tax, provisions already held, growth in RWA, and the fact that Tier 2 capital absorbs losses only in a gone-concern state. Use it as an order of magnitude to frame how large a transition shock would have to be, not as a result. Caution on the NPA decline. Of the reduction in SCB gross NPAs in 2024-25 (₹2.75 lakh crore), write-offs were ₹1.58 lakh crore, about 57%; recoveries and upgradations were 42.8% (para IV.29). Write-offs improve the GNPA ratio without recovering money, so treat ”multi-decadal low” with care when you measure credit quality."},{"pdf":107,"printed":92,"ch":4,"text":"6.2 Where the credit goes (sectoral deployment)\nTable IV.16 (p. 67) gives SCB credit by sector at end-March 2025 (total bank credit ₹1,82,43,972 crore): Table 4.17. RBI, Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India (2007-: 6.2 Where the credit goes (sectoral deployment) Sector\n\n₹ crore\n\nShare of bank credit (my\ncalculation)\n\nAgriculture and allied\n\n22,87,060\n\n12.5%\n\nIndustry (micro, small, medium, large)\n\n39,85,660\n\n21.8%\n\nof which large industry\n\n28,23,942\n\n15.5%\n\nServices\n\n50,93,565\n\n27.9%\n\nof which commercial real estate\n\n5,23,264\n\n2.9%\n\nof which NBFCs\n\n16,35,102\n\n9.0%\n\nPersonal loans\n\n59,71,696\n\n32.7%\n\nof which housing\n\n30,10,477\n\n16.5%\n\nThree points matter for transition risk.\n1. The report does not give a carbon-intensive breakdown. Industry is shown in size classes, and the industry-wise GNPA data in Charts IV.23 and IV.24 (p. 68 to 69) are graphics, not numbers I could extract. Sector-level exposure to power, steel, cement and oil and gas has to come from RBI's separate industry-wise credit releases or bank disclosures.\n2. Lending to NBFCs is an indirect channel. Banks lent ₹16.35 lakh crore to NBFCs (Table IV.16). The same report's Appendix Table VI.5 (p. 188) shows that upper- and middle-layer NBFCs had ₹12.86 lakh crore of advances to power, 56% of their industry credit and about 27% of their gross advances (my calculation). A power-sector shock can therefore reach banks through NBFCs. I have not checked what the table's ”Power” row includes.\n3. Real estate is both a transition and a physical-risk channel. Housing is 16.5% of bank credit and commercial real estate 2.9%; the Basel FAQ on climate and property values (Section 2, p. 206) applies directly.\n\n6.3 How we got here: Basel and the NPA cycle\n• Basel II. Indian banks with foreign operations moved to the Basel II framework on 31 March 2008 and all other commercial banks (except RRBs and local area banks) by 31 March 2009; the minimum CRAR stayed at 9% (2007-08 edition, para 2.85, p. 44).\n• Basel III. ”The Basel III capital regulation has been implemented in India from April 1, 2013 in phases and will be fully implemented as on March 31, 2018” (2012-13 edition, para 1.20, p. 7). RBI estimated that public sector banks would need additional capital of ₹4.15 trillion, of which ₹1.4 to 1.5 trillion would be equity (para 1.21).\n• The bad-loan cycle. The GNPA ratio of SCBs rose for seven consecutive years until 2018-19, as ”recognition of bad loans neared completion” (2018-19 edition, IV.29, p. 50)."},{"pdf":108,"printed":93,"ch":4,"text":"For PSBs it reached 14.6% in 2017-18, with large borrowal accounts at 23.1% (2017-18 edition, IV.39 and IV.44, p. 60 to 61). Compare 2.6% in 2024-25. This history is useful as an empirical benchmark: it shows how large an asset-quality shock Indian PSBs have already absorbed, and that the recovery relied on recapitalisation, the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code and write-offs rather than on organic capital generation. I read only the headline sentences for 2017-18 and 2018-19, so verify before you build an argument on the PSB recapitalisation amount.\n\n6.4 What RBI says about climate risk, year by year\nThe series is the best single source for dating RBI's climate policy path. Each item below was read in the edition named. Table 4.18. RBI, Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India (2007-: 6.4 What RBI says about climate risk, year by year Edition\n\nWhat it records\n\nPage\n\n2021-22\n\nRBI joined the Network for Greening the Financial System on 23 April\n2021; set up a Sustainable Finance Group in May 2021; surveyed banks\nin January 2022 (para III.26).\n\n2021-22\n\nDiscussion paper on climate risk and sustainable finance released in July 6\n2022, recommending that regulated entities embed climate risk in\nstrategy, governance and risk management (para I.22).\n\n2021-22, Box\nIII.2\n\nSurvey results, released 27 July 2022, covering 12 PSBs, 16 private\nbanks and 6 foreign banks (see quotes below).\n\n2022-23\n\nFramework for green deposits issued 11 April 2023, with allocation to\nnine categories and third-party verification (para III.35).\n\n2023-24\n\nDraft disclosure framework on climate-related financial risks released 28\nFebruary 2024, built on four pillars (governance, strategy, risk\nmanagement, metrics and targets) (para III.21, Box III.2).\n\n2023-24\n\nRBI is finalising a guidance note on scenario analysis and stress testing\nof climate risks, and plans RB-CRIS (paras I.34, I.35).\n\n2024-25\n\nRBI is ”following a building block approach” to climate regulation;\nRB-CRIS operationalisation ”is underway” (para III.47). RB-CRIS was\nannounced in October 2024.\n\n2024-25\n\nClimate finance section: RBI describes physical and transition risks that 10\n”may affect credit, market, liquidity, and operational risks” (para I.48).\n\nQuotable (2021-22 Box III.2, p. 32): ”Board-level engagement on climate risk and sustainable finance is inadequate.” Quotable (2021-22 Box III.2, p. 32): ”Most banks also felt that the available data were insufficient for an appropriate assessment of climate-related financial risks and the processes and methodologies to measure and monitor these risks were also not sufficiently developed.” Quotable (2021-22 Box III.2, p. 32): ”A majority of banks have not aligned their climaterelated financial disclosures with any internationally accepted framework.”"},{"pdf":109,"printed":94,"ch":4,"text":"Quotable (2023-24, I.32, p. 7): ”Climate change risks are envisaged to impact profitability of financial institutions, growth prospects, and inflation dynamics and, thus, impinge upon financial stability and price stability.” RBI adds that supervision needs ”enhanced risk management guidelines; disclosure requirements; periodic stress testing; and stipulating reasonable verification and assurance functions.” Quotable (2023-24, Box III.2, p. 33): ”Currently, REs are required to disclose information on material risks as a part of their Pillar 3 disclosures.” Quotable (2024-25, III.47, p. 38): ”The regulatory landscape for climate change risks is evolving considering the global developments as well as the maturity of the domestic ecosystem. In this regard, the Reserve Bank is following a building block approach, focusing on development of specific capacities and technical expertise in the REs.”\n\nWhat this tells you. As of the 2024-25 report, India's climate regime consists of disclosure and data initiatives, a green deposits framework and guidance in preparation. There is no climate-specific capital requirement, which matches the Basel Framework's position (Section 2) and the RBI capital circular (Section 3). That gap is your thesis's space.\n\n6.5 How this feeds the thesis\n• Baseline data: PSB capital, NPA and provisioning levels at end-March 2025 (Tables IV.5, IV.9), for calibrating the starting point of a stress test.\n• Context for scenario size: the 2017-18 PSB GNPA peak of 14.6% shows a historical shock of known size.\n• Policy timeline: the dated climate milestones in Section 6.4, citable without needing the original circulars.\n• Limits: bank-group aggregates only; no carbon-intensive sector split; no bank-level data. For those you will need RBI's Statistical Tables Relating to Banks in India, sector-wise credit releases, or each bank's annual report (not in your folder).\n\n7. Master quotables list and how Layer 3 feeds the thesis\n7.1 Capital thresholds side by side\nTable 4.19. Master quotables list and how Layer 3 feeds the thesis: 7.1 Capital thresholds side by side Hull (2015 text)\n\nRequirement\n\nBasel (global)\n\nRBI (India)\n\nMinimum CET1\n\n4.5% (RBC20.1, p. 139)\n\n5.5% (4.2.2(ii), p. 11)\n\n4.5% (p.\n358)\n\nMinimum Tier 1\n\n7% (p. 11)\n\nMinimum total capital\n\n9% (p. 11 to 12)\n\nConservation buffer\n\n2.5% CET1 (RBC30.2, p. 157)\n\n2.5% CET1 (p. 12)\n\n2.5% (p.\n358)\n\nTotal capital plus\nconservation buffer\n\n10.5%\n\n11.5% (p. 12)\n\n10.5% (p.\n359)"},{"pdf":110,"printed":95,"ch":4,"text":"Hull (2015\ntext)\n\nRequirement\n\nBasel (global)\n\nRBI (India)\n\nCountercyclical buffer\n\n0 to 2.5% (RBC30.9, p. 160)\n\n0 to 2.5% (17.2.1, p. 181) 0 to 2.5% (p.\n359)\n\nLeverage ratio\n\n3% in Hull's text; see the\nFramework's LEV chapter (not\nread)\n\n4% D-SIBs, 3.5% others\n(16.2.2, p. 167)\n\n3% (p. 360)\n\nRBI's 2024-25 Trend and Progress report confirms the Indian figures as in force in 2025 (IV.24, p. 57). Because the RBI circular has been replaced (Section 3), re-check the Indian figures in the November 2025 Capital Adequacy Directions before quoting them as current.\n\n7.2 The ten quotes to know\nTable 4.20. Master quotables list and how Layer 3 feeds the thesis: 7.2 The ten quotes to know\n\nQuote (short form)\n\nSource and page\n\n”The range of economic conditions or\nunexpected events that should be\nconsidered ... should include\nclimate-related financial risks, both\nphysical and transition risks.”\n\nBasel Framework,\nRegulatory basis for\nCRE36 FAQ, PDF p. putting transition risk in\n317\ncredit assessment\n\nBanks ”should iteratively and\nprogressively consider climate-related\nfinancial risks ... in their stress testing\nprocesses.”\n\nBasel Framework,\nLegitimacy of a climate\nCRE36 FAQ, PDF p. stress test\n322\n\nAdd ”a margin of conservatism due to\ndata deficiencies, such as poor data\nquality or scarce climate-related data.”\n\nBasel Framework,\nLimitations section\nCRE36 FAQ, PDF p.\n326\n\nBanks should check whether ”the current\nmarket value incorporates the potential\nchanges in the value of properties\nemerging from climate-related financial\nrisks.”\n\nBasel Framework,\nCollateral and LGD\nCRE20 FAQ, PDF p. channel\n206\n\nRBI T&P 2024-25,\n”In India, the minimum ... CRAR ... is\nset at 9.0 per cent [11.5 per cent inclusive IV.24, p. 57\nof capital conservation buffer] ... both\none percentage point above the Basel III\nnorms.”\n\nDefining the adequacy\nbenchmark\n\n”The regulatory landscape for climate\nchange risks is evolving ... the Reserve\nBank is following a building block\napproach.”\n\nRBI T&P 2024-25,\nIII.47, p. 38\n\nPolicy context and gap\n\n”Board-level engagement on climate risk\nand sustainable finance is inadequate.”\n\nRBI T&P 2021-22,\nBox III.2, p. 32\n\nEvidence of low\npreparedness\n\n”System looks strongest precisely when it Borio, Drehmann\nis most vulnerable” (paradox of financial and Tsatsaronis\ninstability).\n(2014), p. 8\n\nUse it for\n\nCaution on reading 2025's\nstrong capital ratios"},{"pdf":111,"printed":96,"ch":4,"text":"Quote (short form)\n\nSource and page\n\nUse it for\n\n”How much equity capital does DLC\nneed? ... hypothesizing an extremely\nadverse scenario and considering whether\nthe bank would survive.”\n\nHull, p. 29\n\nSimple logic of any capital\nstress test\n\n”defaults and recoveries to some extent\nare influenced by the same underlying\nsystematic risk drivers so that they can\nnot be independent.”\n\nBluhm et al., p. 21\n\nShocking PD and LGD\ntogether\n\nCheck before use. Quote 8 is a paraphrase from my notes of Borio et al.; take the exact wording and page from Section 1. Quotes 1 to 4 are the Basel Committee's FAQ wording and are labelled by PDF page because the file has no printed page numbers.\n\n7.3 How Layer 3 connects to the other layers\n• From Layer 1 and 2 (scenarios, discounting). Layer 3 supplies the receiving end of the chain: how a scenario's economic shock becomes a loss and then a capital ratio.\n• To Layer 4 (NGFS and climate stress tests). The Borio et al. warning about\ncontagion models (Section 1) and the Basel position that climate is a driver of credit risk (Section 2) frame how to read the central-bank scenarios.\n• To Layer 6 (your model). Section 5 (CreditPortfolioView and the one-factor model) and Section 4.3 (the IRB formula) give the modelling skeleton; Section 6.1 gives the starting values for Indian PSBs.\n\n7.4 The transmission chain the thesis tests\n1. A transition shock changes borrower cash flows and collateral values in carbon-intensive sectors.\n2. Ratings, PDs and LGDs move together (Bluhm p. 21; Basel p. 317 and 326).\n3. Provisions rise and NPAs are reclassified; risk weights on NPAs increase (RBI circular 5.12, p. 45).\n4. Capital falls while RWA rises, so CRAR moves toward the 11.5% threshold (RBI, p. 12).\n5. The cushion available is large in 2025 (PSB CRAR 16.1%, Section 6.1), so the question becomes how large a shock is needed, and whether the strong starting position hides vulnerability (Borio et al., p. 8).\n\n7.5 What I did not read, and flags\n• Replaced document: the RBI Basel III Master Circular (1 April 2025) in your folder is marked withdrawn; RBI's list shows new Capital Adequacy Directions dated 28 November\n2025. I could not open the new text, so confirm figures and paragraph numbers there.\n• Basel Framework (1,429 pages): I read the capital minimums, buffers, IRB opening chapters and all climate FAQs. Not read: capital definition details, market and operational risk, securitisation, counterparty risk, liquidity, large exposures and the supervisory review chapters."},{"pdf":112,"printed":97,"ch":4,"text":"• Hull (743 pages): four chapters or sections of 28 chapters. Bluhm (385 pages): Chapter 1, the CreditPortfolioView section and the start of the one-factor model.\n• RBI Trend and Progress (18 files): the 2024-25 edition in detail; other years only at cited passages. 2014-15 and 2015-16 are partial files.\n• Not in your folder but needed later: bank-level capital and NPA data, sector-wise credit to carbon-intensive industries, and the new RBI Capital Adequacy Directions."},{"pdf":113,"printed":98,"ch":4,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 3"},{"pdf":114,"printed":99,"ch":4,"text":""},{"pdf":115,"printed":100,"ch":4,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 3"},{"pdf":116,"printed":101,"ch":4,"text":""}],"cards":[]};

window.CHTML=window.CHTML||{};window.CHTML.ln=window.CHTML.ln||{};window.CHTML.ln[4]={"h":"<h1 data-b=\"1\" id=\"b1\">Before you start: what you need to know first</h1>\n<p data-b=\"2\">Layer 3 is where the thesis stops being about climate and starts being about banks. It has fewer prerequisites than it looks, but the vocabulary is dense and every later layer assumes it. Learn these seven ideas first; the sources in this layer are written for people who already know them.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"3\" id=\"b3\">1. The bank balance sheet</h2>\n<p data-b=\"4\">A bank’ s assets are mostly loans and investments. Its liabilities are mostly deposits and borrowings. The gap is <strong>equity capital</strong>, which is the cushion that absorbs losses before depositors are hurt. A bank with a thin cushion relative to its assets is highly <strong>leveraged</strong>.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"5\" id=\"b5\">2. Risk-weighted assets and the capital ratio</h2>\n<p data-b=\"6\">Regulators do not compare capital with total assets alone. They scale each exposure by a <strong>risk weight</strong> to get <strong>risk-weighted assets (RWA)</strong>, and then compare capital with RWA:</p>\n<p data-b=\"7\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Capital adequacy ratio (CAR)</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">eligible regulatory capital</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">risk-weighted assets</mtext></mfrac></mrow></math></p>\n<p data-b=\"8\">A riskier loan carries a higher weight, so it needs more capital behind it. This ratio is the dependent variable your thesis is ultimately about: how climate risk moves the numerator (through losses) and the denominator (through higher risk weights or credit deterioration).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"9\" id=\"b9\">3. Tiers of capital</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"10\"><strong>Common Equity Tier 1 (CET1):</strong> the highest-quality capital, mainly paid-in equity and retained earnings.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"11\"><strong>Additional Tier 1 (AT1):</strong> loss-absorbing instruments that can be written off or converted.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"12\"><strong>Tier 2:</strong> lower-quality capital that absorbs loss in a gone-concern case.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"13\">The regulation sets a minimum for each and adds <strong>buffers</strong> on top. The exact percentages for India are in the RBI section below.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"14\" id=\"b14\">4. Credit-risk parameters: PD, LGD, EAD</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"15\"><strong>Probability of default (PD):</strong> the chance a borrower defaults within a year.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"16\"><strong>Loss given default (LGD):</strong> the share of the exposure lost if default happens.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"17\"><strong>Exposure at default (EAD):</strong> the amount owed at the time of default.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"18\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Expected loss (EL)</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">PD</mtext><mo>×</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">LGD</mtext><mo>×</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">EAD</mtext></mrow></math></p>\n<p data-b=\"19\">Expected loss is meant to be covered by provisions and pricing. <strong>Capital is held for unexpected loss</strong>, the deviation from the expected value in bad years. A climate shock can raise PD and LGD for exposed borrowers (higher expected loss) and also widen the spread of outcomes (higher unexpected loss). Keep the two apart.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"20\" id=\"b20\">5. Standardised versus internal-ratings approaches</h2>\n<p data-b=\"21\">Under the <strong>standardised approach</strong> regulators fix the risk weights by exposure type and external rating. Under the <strong>internal ratings-based (IRB)</strong> approach, banks estimate PD (and, in the advanced version, LGD and EAD) themselves, subject to supervisory approval. Which one a bank uses determines how credit deterioration reaches its RWA.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"22\" id=\"b22\">6. Asset quality and provisioning</h2>\n<p data-b=\"23\">An <strong>NPA</strong> (non-performing asset) is a loan that has stopped paying. <strong>Provisions</strong> are charges against profit that build a reserve for expected losses. Gross NPA ratios, provisioning coverage and slippages are the standard indicators of asset quality, and they are what the Reserve Bank’ s annual reports track.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"24\" id=\"b24\">7. The three pillars and the Basel generations</h2>\n<p data-b=\"25\">The Basel framework has three pillars: <strong>Pillar 1</strong> minimum capital for credit, market and operational risk; <strong>Pillar 2</strong> supervisory review and a bank-specific capital add-on; <strong>Pillar 3</strong> public disclosure. Basel I (1988) introduced risk weights, Basel II (2004) added internal models, and Basel III (after the 2008 crisis) raised the quality and quantity of capital and added buffers and a leverage ratio. India follows Basel III with its own, stricter, thresholds.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"26\" id=\"b26\">Map of Layer 3</h2>\n<table data-b=\"27\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.1.</strong> Before you start: Map of Layer 3</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Section</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Source</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">One-line job</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Borio, Drehmann and Tsatsaronis (2014)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">What macro stress tests can and cannot do, and how to run one honestly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Basel Framework (BCBS)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The international rulebook: definitions, ratios, buffers, credit-risk weights.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RBI Basel III Capital Regulations (2025)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">India’ s version: the actual minimums and the weights Indian banks face.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Hull, <em>Risk Management and Financial Institutions</em></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The textbook explanation of how banks measure and manage risk, and how capital rules work.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bluhm, Overbeck and Wagner, <em>An Introduction to Credit Risk Modeling</em></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The mathematics of PD, LGD, default correlation and portfolio loss.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RBI <em>Trend and Progress of Banking in India</em></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">What the actual numbers look like for Indian public sector banks, 2008 to 2025.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Master list</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Quotes and how Layer 3 feeds the thesis.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"28\"><strong>Suggested reading order:</strong> Borio (13 pages) first, then the RBI regulations (which are readable and specific), then the Basel Framework as a reference, then Hull and Bluhm only for the chapters that matter to you, then the <em>Trend and Progress</em> reports for the Indian data.</p>\n<p data-b=\"29\"><strong>How Layer 3 connects to the rest.</strong> Layers 1 and 2 give the climate shock and its policy price. Layer 3 gives the bank’ s capital arithmetic. Layer 4 asks how losses spread between banks. Layers 5 and 6 test how markets and regulators price the whole chain. The climate-to-capital link itself is my framing of the thesis question; the sources in this layer do not mention climate.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"30\" id=\"b30\">1. Borio, Drehmann and Tsatsaronis (2014), Stress-testing macro stress testing</h1>\n<p data-b=\"31\"><strong>Source:</strong> Borio, C., Drehmann, M. and Tsatsaronis, K. (2014), \"Stress-testing macro stress testing: Does it live up to expectations?\", <em>Journal of Financial Stability</em> 12: 3–15 (your file \"Borio, Drehmann &amp; Tsatsaronis (2014).pdf\"). The authors are at the Bank for International Settlements. <strong>Correction to my map above:</strong> this paper is not about the credit-to-GDP gap or capital ratios; it reviews what macro stress tests can and cannot do. That makes it more useful for your thesis than the description I gave. Page references are the journal pages (3 to 15).</p>\n<p data-b=\"32\"><strong>What I read.</strong> The full text, including both annexes.</p>\n<p data-b=\"33\">A macro stress test applies a hypothetical adverse scenario to the financial system as a whole to see whether banks keep enough capital. Any climate stress test you build or cite is a member of this family, so the limitations in this paper apply to your own work.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"34\" id=\"b34\">Vocabulary</h2>\n<table data-b=\"35\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.2.</strong> Borio, Drehmann and Tsatsaronis (2014): Vocabulary</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Term</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Macro stress test</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A test of the stability of the financial system, or a part of it, under a hypothetical adverse scenario (p. 4).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Four elements</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Risk exposures, the scenario (exogenous shocks), the model that maps shocks to impact, and a measure of the outcome (p. 4).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Severe yet plausible\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The usual rule for scenarios: severe enough to matter, plausible enough to be taken seriously (p. 4).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Top-down / bottom-up</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Top-down: the authority runs its own model. Bottom-up: banks run the scenario in their own models and the authority aggregates (p. 5).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Reverse stress test</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Start from a failure outcome and search for the shocks that cause it (p. 4–5).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Paradox of financial instability</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The system looks strongest precisely when it is most vulnerable (p. 8).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Financial cycle</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The medium-term joint movement of credit and property prices; crises tend to begin near its peak (p. 7).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"36\" id=\"b36\">The argument: five propositions</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"37\"><strong>Macro stress testing is a toolbox, not a single tool (p. 4–5).</strong> Tests differ in which institutions and risks they cover, in scenarios, models and outcome measures. Most cover credit risk in the banking book, and the current standard horizon is two to three years (p. 5).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"38\"><strong>Beware of macro stress tests as early warning devices (p. 6–9).</strong> \"To our knowledge, no macro stress test carried out ahead of the crisis identified the build-up of vulnerabilities\" (p. 6). Two reasons. First, the models are partial equilibrium with weak feedbacks and are mis-specified, so the scenarios have to be \"unreasonably\" large to produce distress (p. 6–7). Second, the context: financial crises tend to begin at the peak of the financial cycle, when indicators look healthiest (p. 7–9).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"39\"><strong>They can greatly help in crisis management and resolution (p. 9).</strong> Once a crisis has erupted, vulnerabilities are visible, initial conditions are weak and prudence has replaced hubris, so \"it is not necessary to shake the system so hard to reveal weaknesses\" (p. 9).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"40\"><strong>Their additional benefits should not be underestimated (p. 9–10).</strong> They force economists, risk managers, loan officers and supervisors onto a common language, expose model outliers and improve data.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"41\"><strong>If you do them, do them right (p. 10–12).</strong> Three elements of good practice: the will to really stress the system; buy-in by stakeholders; and a clear follow-up plan (p. 10). Use conservative loss estimates and severe scenarios, not overly constrained by history (p. 10). Consider leading indicators such as credit-to-GDP and property-price gaps and the debt-service ratio to decide when to run harder tests (p. 11). Pay more attention to common exposures than to bilateral interlinkages (p. 11–12).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"42\" id=\"b42\">Four points that matter for your thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"43\"><strong>No error bands.</strong> \"Stress tests generally report no error bands around their forecasts but only point estimates\" (p. 6, fn 7). The point-estimate impression is false precision, the same worry that Pindyck raises about IAMs in Layer 2.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"44\"><strong>Non-linearity.</strong> Most models are estimated \"as if the true world behaved in a log-linear fashion, when in fact it does not\" (p. 6). In a severe case this understates losses.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"45\"><strong>Debt-service burden.</strong> In the annex (p. 13), \"as the costs of debt service relative to income increase, bank losses become more sensitive to a deteriorating macroeconomic environment.\" Borrower leverage amplifies shocks.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"46\"><strong>A warning for Layer 4.</strong> The authors say \"too much attention is being paid to bilateral interlinkages and network analysis\" and that a crisis \"acts like a tsunami that sweeps away all before it, not like a force that knocks down one domino after another\" (p. 11–12). That is a direct challenge to the contagion-network papers in Layer 4, so expect to argue with it.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"47\" id=\"b47\">Quotables</h2>\n<table data-b=\"48\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.3.</strong> Borio, Drehmann and Tsatsaronis (2014): Quotables</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote (short, verbatim)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it could be used</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"macro stress tests are ill-suited as early warning devices\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3 (abstract)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stating the limits of any stress test, including yours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"stress tests turned out to be part of the problem. They lulled policymakers and market participants into a false sense of security.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Arguing for scepticism towards reassuring results</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"No matter how hard one shook the box, little would drop out.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Explaining why models with weak amplification need huge shocks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The very essence of financial instability is that normal-size shocks cause the system to break down.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7–8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Contrasting with exogenous-shock stress tests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the system looks strongest precisely when it is most vulnerable\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Warning that healthy current ratios do not prove resilience</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the severity of the scenarios could be increased based on the signals from reduced-form leading indicators of financial distress\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">12</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Justifying a severe-case design when warning indicators flash</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"A financial crisis acts like a tsunami that sweeps away all before it, not like a force that knocks down one domino after another\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">12</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Debating network-contagion models (Layer 4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"No stress test can succeed unless there is a strong will to stress the system hard, to distrust rosy results and to be sceptical about seemingly strong initial conditions.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">12</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Closing statement on method</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"49\" id=\"b49\">What it means for your thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"50\"><strong>How to describe your own stress test honestly.</strong> Say it is a conditional scenario analysis in the four-element format (exposures, scenario, model, outcome), say what is exogenous, and say what is left out (feedbacks, liquidity, second-round effects). This pre-empts the objections this paper makes.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"51\"><strong>Timing and horizon.</strong> The standard is two to three years (p. 5). Climate transition plays out over decades, so you need to state how you reconcile that horizon with the one used in your results (my observation; the paper says nothing about climate).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"52\"><strong>Capital targets as absolute amounts.</strong> The authors, citing Greenlaw et al., suggest setting capital targets in absolute amounts rather than as ratios to assets or risk-weighted assets, to limit an unintended credit crunch (p. 10). If your thesis ends with a capital shortfall, consider reporting it in rupees as well as in ratio points.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"53\"><strong>India.</strong> The paper does not mention India. It does say exchange rate risk may be more important for emerging markets (p. 4, fn 2).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"54\"><strong>Can you skip the original?</strong> For the main argument, yes. It is only 13 pages and written in plain language, so I would still read Proposition 2 (p. 6–9) and Section 7 (p. 10–12) yourself because they set out the limitations you will need to cite. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"55\" id=\"b55\">2. Basel Committee, The Basel Framework (consolidated standards, generated 15 October 2026)</h1>\n<p data-b=\"56\"><strong>Source:</strong> Basel Committee on Banking Supervision, <em>The Basel Framework</em>, the full consolidated set of standards (your file \"basel-framework-full.pdf\", 1,429 pages). Page references below are the <strong>PDF page numbers</strong> (\"N / 1429\" in the footer), because the document is a web export with no printed page numbers. It is a living text, so cite it with the retrieval date.</p>\n<p data-b=\"57\"><strong>What I read.</strong> The contents; the minimum capital chapter (RBC20); the capital conservation and countercyclical buffers (RBC30); the IRB overview and risk-weight chapter openings (CRE30, CRE31); and <strong>every one of the 117 passages mentioning climate</strong>, which sit in FAQ boxes spread through the credit-risk chapters. I did not read the market-risk, operational-risk, liquidity, large-exposure, securitisation or counterparty chapters, nor the definition of capital (CAP) in detail. Several formulas in the PDF are images, so I describe them in words and leave the algebra to Section 4 (Hull) and Section 5 (Bluhm).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"58\" id=\"b58\">Why this source matters most for your thesis</h2>\n<p data-b=\"59\">The Basel Framework is where the global rules for bank capital live. Its credit-risk chapters now carry a set of <strong>official FAQs on climate-related financial risks</strong> (the CRE20 change log says they were added on 8 December 2022 and updated with FAQs published on 10 June 2025). They tell banks, in the Committee’ s own words, that climate risk must be handled <em>inside</em> the existing credit-risk rules rather than through a new capital charge. That is the regulatory frame for a thesis on transition risk and capital adequacy.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"60\" id=\"b60\">Vocabulary</h2>\n<table data-b=\"61\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.4.</strong> Basel Committee, The Basel Framework (consolidated standards: Vocabulary</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Term</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Pillar 1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Minimum capital for credit, market and operational risk.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">CET1 / Tier 1 / Total capital</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Core equity-type capital; Tier 1 including additional Tier 1 instruments; total including Tier 2.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Conservation buffer</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Extra CET1 above the minimum; breach restricts dividends and bonuses, not operations (RBC30, p. 157).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Countercyclical buffer</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Extra CET1, 0 to 2.5% of RWA, set by national authorities in credit booms (RBC30, p. 160).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Standardised approach (SA)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Risk weights set by the regulator, mostly by external rating or exposure type (CRE20 to CRE22).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IRB approach</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bank estimates PD, LGD, EAD and maturity; a regulatory formula turns them into capital (CRE30 to CRE36).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Slotting</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Supervisory categories for specialised lending such as project finance (CRE33).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Expected vs unexpected loss</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Expected loss is covered by provisions; capital covers unexpected loss (CRE30, p. 251).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"62\" id=\"b62\">2.1 Minimum capital and buffers (global)</h2>\n<table data-b=\"63\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.5.</strong> Basel Committee, The Basel Framework (consolidated standards: 2.1 Minimum capital and buffers (global)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Requirement</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Basel minimum</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">CET1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4.5% of RWA</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RBC20.1, p. 139</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Tier 1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RBC20.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Total capital</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RBC20.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Conservation buffer (CET1)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2.5%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RBC30.2, p. 157; footnote to RBC20.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Countercyclical buffer</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0 to 2.5%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RBC30.9, p. 160</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"64\">Compare with India (Section 3): RBI sets total capital at 9% and CET1 at 5.5%, so the Indian stack is 1 percentage point higher at each of CET1, Tier 1 and total capital.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"65\"><strong>Quotable (RBC20.1, p. 139):</strong> \"Banks must meet the following requirements at all times: Common Equity Tier 1 must be at least 4.5% of risk-weighted assets (RWA). Tier 1 capital must be at least 6% of RWA. Total capital must be at least 8.0% of RWA.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"66\"><strong>Quotable (RBC30.2, p. 157):</strong> The conservation buffer of 2.5% is \"established above the regulatory minimum capital requirement. Capital distribution constraints will be imposed on a bank when capital levels fall within this range.\" The constraints \"only relate to distributions, not the operation of the bank.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"67\"><strong>Quotable (RBC30.9, p. 160):</strong> National authorities facing \"a period of excess credit growth ... leading to the build up of system-wide risk\" may require a countercyclical buffer that \"will vary between zero and 2.5% of risk weighted assets.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"68\" id=\"b68\">2.2 The two ways to compute credit risk RWAs</h2>\n<p data-b=\"69\"><strong>Standardised.</strong> The regulator fixes the risk weight by exposure class and, for corporates and banks, by external rating. The bank’ s own judgement matters little, except through a duty of due diligence (below).</p>\n<p data-b=\"70\"><strong>Internal ratings-based.</strong> The bank estimates a probability of default (PD), loss given default (LGD), exposure at default (EAD) and, for corporates, effective maturity, and a prescribed formula converts them into a capital requirement. The IRB design splits loss in two: \"The risk-weight functions ... produce capital requirements for the UL portion. Expected losses are treated separately, as outlined in CRE35\" (CRE30.2, p. 251). Defaulted exposures are handled separately: capital is the greater of zero and LGD minus the bank’ s best estimate of expected loss (CRE31.3, p. 264).</p>\n<p data-b=\"71\"><strong>Relevance to India.</strong> The RBI circular in Section 3 sets out the standardised approach in detail; I have not checked which Indian banks have supervisory approval for IRB (verify this before you rely on either route). Either way, the IRB climate FAQs are your guide to <em>best practice</em> and to how a PD-based stress test would work, not to current Indian rules.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"72\" id=\"b72\">2.3 The climate-related FAQs: a guided map</h2>\n<p data-b=\"73\">The FAQs are short and all point the same way. I list them by the part of the credit-risk process they touch.</p>\n<table data-b=\"74\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.6.</strong> Basel Committee, The Basel Framework (consolidated standards: 2.3 The climate-related FAQs: a guided map</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">What the FAQ says</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">PDF page</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SA due diligence (CRE20)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Banks should integrate climate risk \"either in their own credit risk assessment or when performing due diligence on external ratings.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">181</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SA exposures to banks (CRE21)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Banks should consider material climate risk across \"the entire credit life cycle, including client due diligence as part of the onboarding process and ongoing monitoring.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">188</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SA real estate (CRE20)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Banks should check \"whether the current market value incorporates the potential changes in the value of properties emerging from climate-related financial risks\" (weather hazards, climate-policy standards, shifts in consumption from transition policies).</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">206</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SA risk weights, supervisors (CRE20)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">When judging if risk weights are too low, supervisors \"should also consider climate-related financial risks.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">204</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Specialised lending slotting (CRE33)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Banks should assess how climate risks affect the slotting category, for example transition risk through \"stability of legal and regulatory environment (risk of change in law)\" and physical risk through force majeure.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">293</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IRB rating assignment (CRE36)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Banks \"should take into consideration material and relevant information on the impact of climate-related financial risks on the borrower’ s financial condition\"; where information is lacking, \"consider if it would be appropriate to take a more conservative approach\"; \"expert judgment\" is allowed.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">316</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IRB rating criteria and horizon (CRE36)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Ratings should use a horizon longer than one year, and the range of events considered \"should include climate-related financial risks, both physical and transition risks, if these materialise as credit risks.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">317</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IRB stress testing (CRE36)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Banks should \"iteratively and progressively consider climate-related financial risks that affect the range of possible future economic conditions in their stress testing processes.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">322</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IRB parameter estimation (CRE36)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Add \"a margin of conservatism due to data deficiencies, such as poor data quality or scarce climate-related data.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">326</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Mapping internal grades to external ratings (CRE36)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Banks should check whether the external agency’ s scale \"reflects material climate-related financial risks\" and adjust if it does not.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">330</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"75\"><strong>Quotable (rating criteria, p. 317):</strong> \"The range of economic conditions or unexpected events that should be considered when making the assessment of a borrower’ s ability to perform should include climate-related financial risks, both physical and transition risks, if these materialise as credit risks.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"76\"><strong>Quotable (stress testing, p. 322):</strong> \"Climate-related financial risks have the potential to impact banks’ credit exposures and banks’ assessment of credit risk, asset impairment and expected credit losses. Banks should iteratively and progressively consider climate-related financial risks that affect the range of possible future economic conditions in their stress testing processes.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"77\"><strong>Quotable (data limits, p. 326):</strong> Challenges in estimating PD, LGD and EAD \"include the range of impact uncertainties, limitations in the availability and relevance of historical data describing the relationship of climate risk drivers to traditional financial risks, and questions around the time horizon.\" Banks \"should add a margin of conservatism due to data deficiencies.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"78\"><strong>Quotable (real estate collateral, p. 206):</strong> Banks should determine \"whether the current market value incorporates the potential changes in the value of properties emerging from climate-related financial risks.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"79\" id=\"b79\">2.4 What this does and does not do</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"80\"><strong>It is guidance on process, not a new capital number.</strong> No FAQ changes a risk weight or adds a climate charge. The Committee’ s position is that climate risk is a <em>driver</em> of credit risk, so it should enter PD, LGD, ratings and collateral values. This is the \"driver, not a separate risk type\" view you will meet again in the NGFS material in Layer 4.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"81\"><strong>It admits the data problem.</strong> The repeated phrases \"iteratively and progressively\", \"conservative\" and \"expert judgment\" signal that regulators accept immature methods. Your thesis can cite this as the regulatory licence for a stylised scenario approach.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"82\"><strong>It names transition risk concretely</strong> (changes in law, climate-policy standards, shifts in consumption), which gives you a regulatory definition to align your \"transition risk\" variable with.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"83\"><strong>Separate Basel documents are not in this file.</strong> The Committee’ s 2022 principles for the management and supervision of climate-related financial risks, and its disclosure framework, are different publications. I have not read them.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"84\" id=\"b84\">2.5 How this feeds the thesis</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"85\"><strong>Legitimacy:</strong> the global standard-setter says climate risk belongs in credit risk assessment and stress testing, so a capital-adequacy stress test is a recognised approach.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"86\"><strong>Mechanism:</strong> transition risk reaches capital through ratings, PD and LGD, collateral values and the stress-test scenario. Those are your model’ s channels.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"87\"><strong>Benchmark:</strong> compare the Basel 4.5/6/8% stack with the RBI 5.5/7/9% stack when you set the adequacy threshold.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"88\"><strong>Limitation to cite:</strong> the Framework itself says historical data are weak for climate risk, which supports using scenario analysis.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h1 data-b=\"89\" id=\"b89\">3. RBI, Master Circular: Basel III Capital Regulations (1 April 2025)</h1>\n<p data-b=\"90\"><strong>Source:</strong> Reserve Bank of India, Master Circular on Basel III Capital Regulations, dated 1 April 2025 (your file \"Basel III Capital Regulations RBI (2025).PDF\"). Page references are the printed page numbers of the circular.</p>\n<p data-b=\"91\"><strong>Important flag: this document has been replaced.</strong> Every page of your copy carries a \"Withdrawn\" watermark. RBI’ s Master Directions page lists <em>Reserve Bank of India (Commercial Banks – Prudential Norms on Capital Adequacy) Directions, 2025</em>, issued 28 November 2025 (notification RBI/DOR/2025-26/151, per a secondary summary), and says earlier Master Circulars \"will stand withdrawn with the issue of the Master Direction on the subject.\" I could not open the new Directions themselves, so I have not checked whether the numbers below changed. <strong>For the thesis:</strong> use this circular for how the framework worked up to November 2025, and cite the 2025 Directions for anything stated as current. Before you quote a figure in the thesis, check it against the new text.</p>\n<p data-b=\"92\"><strong>What I read.</strong> The table of contents, Part A sections 4 and 5 (capital composition, minima, and credit-risk weights), the leverage ratio and countercyclical buffer sections, and the ICAAP stress-testing paragraph. I did not read the market-risk, operational-risk, counterparty-risk or securitisation sections, or the annexes. <strong>The circular contains no climate or ESG content</strong> (I searched the full text). That is itself a useful fact: climate risk entered Indian bank capital rules, if at all, only through the general channels below.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"93\" id=\"b93\">Vocabulary</h2>\n<table data-b=\"94\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.7.</strong> RBI, Master Circular: Basel III Capital Regulations (1 April 2025): Vocabulary</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Term</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">CRAR</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Capital to Risk-weighted Assets Ratio, RBI’ s name for the total capital ratio (p. 11).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">CET1 / AT1 / Tier 2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Common Equity Tier 1; Additional Tier 1; Tier 2. Tier 1 is \"going-concern\" capital and Tier 2 \"gone-concern\" capital (p. 11, footnote 5).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">CCB</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Capital Conservation Buffer, 2.5% of RWAs, held in CET1 (p. 12).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">CCCB</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Countercyclical Capital Buffer, 0 to 2.5% of RWAs, in CET1 (p. 181).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ICAAP / SREP</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bank’ s own internal capital assessment; RBI’ s supervisory review of it (Pillar 2).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Leverage ratio</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Capital divided by total (non-risk-weighted) exposure (p. 167).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"95\" id=\"b95\">3.1 The minimum capital stack (p. 11–12)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"96\">The ratio is computed as eligible capital over the sum of credit, market and operational risk RWAs. The circular states it in three lines: CET1 ratio, Tier 1 ratio and Total Capital (CRAR), each with the same denominator \"Credit Risk RWA + Market Risk RWA + Operational Risk RWA\" (p. 11).</p>\n<table data-b=\"97\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.8.</strong> RBI, Master Circular: Basel III Capital Regulations (1 April 2025): 3.1 The minimum capital stack (p. 11–12)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Layer</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">% of RWAs</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Minimum CET1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5.5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">p. 12 table, row (i)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Capital conservation buffer (CET1)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2.5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">row (ii)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">CET1 plus CCB</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8.0</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">row (iii)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Additional Tier 1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1.5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">row (iv)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Minimum Tier 1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7.0</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">row (v)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Tier 2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2.0</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">row (vi)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Minimum total capital</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">9.0</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">row (vii)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Minimum total capital plus CCB</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>11.5</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">row (viii)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"98\"><strong>Why India’ s 9% differs from the Basel 8%.</strong> The Basel Committee’ s minimum is 8%. RBI requires 9% (4.2.2(i), p. 11: \"As a matter of prudence ... a minimum total capital (MTC) of 9% of total risk weighted assets\"). That is one reason Indian banks usually report CRAR well above the Basel floor, which matters when you interpret \"capital adequacy\" in your dependent variable.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"99\"><strong>Quotable (p. 11):</strong> \"Banks shall maintain a minimum Pillar 1 Capital to Risk-weighted Assets Ratio (CRAR) of 9% on an on-going basis (other than capital conservation buffer and countercyclical capital buffer etc.).\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"100\"><strong>Quotable (p. 11):</strong> The Reserve Bank \"will consider prescribing a higher level of minimum capital ratio for each bank under the Pillar 2 framework on the basis of their respective risk profiles and their risk management systems.\" Pillar 2 is the legal hook for a bank-specific climate add-on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"101\" id=\"b101\">3.2 Risk weights: where credit risk and climate risk would meet</h2>\n<p data-b=\"102\">Under the standardised approach the risk weight depends on the counterparty or the product. These are the weights that matter for a public sector bank’ s loan book.</p>\n<table data-b=\"103\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.9.</strong> RBI, Master Circular: Basel III Capital Regulations (1 April 2025): 3.2 Risk weights: where credit risk and climate risk would meet</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Exposure</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Risk weight</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Central government claims</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5.2.1 (page not checked)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Corporates and NBFCs, long term, rated AAA / AA / A / BBB / BB and below</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">20 / 30 / 50 / 100 / 150%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Table 5 Part A, p. 40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Unrated corporates</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">100%; 150% if aggregate banking-system exposure exceeds ₹200 crore</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Table 5, notes (iii), p. 40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Previously rated, now unrated, exposure above ₹100 crore</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">150%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Table 5, note (ii), p. 40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Individual housing loans up to ₹30 lakh, LTV up to 80%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">35%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Table 7, p. 43</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Commercial real estate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">100% (residential CRE 75%)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Table 7, p. 43; 5.11.2, p. 44</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Consumer credit (excluding housing, education, vehicle, gold)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">125%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5.13.3, p. 46</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Capital market exposures</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">125% or rating-based, whichever is higher</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5.13.4, p. 46</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"104\">Two points follow for the thesis. First, most Indian corporate borrowers are unrated, so they sit at a flat 100%. A climate-exposed firm and a climate-safe firm with the same rating carry the same weight, which means <strong>the standardised approach has no direct channel for climate risk</strong>. Second, risk weights move only when a rating changes or a loan turns non-performing. Transition risk reaches bank capital through those two events.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"105\"><strong>Quotable (5.8.1, p. 39–40):</strong> Claims on corporates \"will be risk weighted as per the ratings assigned by the rating agencies registered with SEBI and accredited by the Reserve Bank of India.\" This ties capital to rating agencies, whose climate treatment is a separate question.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"106\" id=\"b106\">3.3 Non-performing assets and capital (5.12, p. 45)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"107\">The unsecured portion of an NPA, net of specific provisions, is weighted by how much has been provided:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"108\">150% if specific provisions are below 20% of the outstanding amount,</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"109\">100% if at least 20%,</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"110\">50% if at least 50% (5.12.1).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"111\">So a deteriorating loan raises RWAs at the same moment provisions reduce capital. This double hit is the mechanism by which a transition shock to borrowers shows up in CRAR. Land, buildings, plant and machinery are <em>not</em> counted as secured collateral for this purpose except in a narrow case with 15% provisions (5.12.3 to 5.12.4, p. 45); that matters for carbon-intensive industrial borrowers whose collateral is heavy plant.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"112\" id=\"b112\">3.4 Buffers, leverage and stress testing</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"113\"><strong>Countercyclical buffer (17.2, p. 181).</strong> It may range from 0 to 2.5% of RWAs, in CET1. The main indicator is the credit-to-GDP gap, \"used in conjunction with GNPA growth\" (17.2.3). The buffer starts at a gap of 3 percentage points and reaches its maximum at 15 (17.2.4). Decisions are normally pre-announced four quarters ahead (17.2.2). This is a worked example of the financial-cycle logic in Borio et al. (Section 1).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"114\"><strong>Leverage ratio (16.2.2, p. 167).</strong> Minimum 4% for domestic systemically important banks (D-SIBs), and 3.5% for others.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"115\"><strong>ICAAP stress tests (12.10, p. 133).</strong> Banks must \"conduct relevant stress tests periodically ... to evaluate the potential vulnerability of the bank to some unlikely but plausible events or movements in the market conditions.\" This is the clause under which a bank, or RBI, can require a climate scenario. It does not mention climate.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"116\"><strong>Quotable (12.10, p. 133):</strong> Stress testing evaluates \"the potential vulnerability of the bank to some unlikely but plausible events\" and can give management \"a better understanding of the bank’ s likely exposure in extreme circumstances.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"117\" id=\"b117\">3.5 How this feeds the thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"118\">It supplies your <strong>definition of capital adequacy</strong>: CRAR with CET1 and buffer components, and the 9% and 11.5% thresholds, are the benchmarks against which a simulated capital loss is judged.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"119\">It supplies the <strong>transmission mechanism</strong>: rating downgrade or NPA classification, higher risk weight, higher provisions, lower capital.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"120\">It supplies the <strong>gap</strong>: the framework has no climate-specific term, so any climate effect has to be modelled through PD, LGD or ratings.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"121\"><strong>Citation caution:</strong> the withdrawn circular is fine as a historical source, but mark it as superseded and cite the 2025 Directions for current rules.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h1 data-b=\"122\" id=\"b122\">4. Hull, Risk Management and Financial Institutions (4th ed., 2015)</h1>\n<p data-b=\"123\"><strong>Source:</strong> Hull, J. C. (2015), <em>Risk Management and Financial Institutions</em>, 4th edition, Wiley (your file \"Hull, Risk Management and Financial Institutions.pdf\", 743 pages). Page references are the book’ s printed pages. This is a textbook, so use it to understand mechanisms and cite the primary source (Basel, RBI) for rules.</p>\n<p data-b=\"124\"><strong>What I read.</strong> Chapter 2 (Section 2.2, a toy bank and its capital), Chapter 15 (Basel I and Basel II, including the IRB formula and the three pillars), Chapter 16 (Section 16.2, Basel III) and Chapter 22 (scenario analysis and stress testing). I did not read the market-risk, VaR, derivatives, liquidity, insurance or operational-risk chapters. <strong>Two cautions:</strong> (1) the 4th edition predates the 2017 finalisation of Basel III, so its Basel III numbers match the 2010 rules, not today’ s; (2) the book contains no climate content.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"125\" id=\"b125\">Why read it</h2>\n<p data-b=\"126\">Hull gives you three things the regulatory texts do not: a worked example of why a bank needs capital, the logic behind the IRB capital formula, and a practical account of how stress tests are built and misused.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"127\" id=\"b127\">Vocabulary</h2>\n<table data-b=\"128\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.10.</strong> Hull, Risk Management and Financial Institutions (4th ed., 2015): Vocabulary</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Term</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ROE</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Return on equity: profit divided by equity capital (p. 29).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">WCDR</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Worst-case default rate: the default rate the bank is 99.9% sure will not be exceeded next year (p. 341).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Gaussian copula</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A model that links obligors’ defaults through one common factor with correlation ρ (p. 340–341).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Foundation vs Advanced IRB</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Foundation: bank supplies PD only. Advanced: bank also supplies LGD, EAD and maturity (p. 343).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Reverse stress test</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Search for the scenario that causes failure (p. 468).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"129\" id=\"b129\">4.1 Why banks need capital: the DLC example (Chapter 2, p. 28–30)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"130\">Hull builds a hypothetical bank, Deposits and Loans Corporation (DLC), with $100 million of assets: $80m loans, $15m cash and securities, $5m fixed assets; funded by $90m deposits, $5m subordinated debt and $5m equity (Table 2.2, p. 28). Loan losses in a normal year are 0.8% of assets (Table 2.3, p. 29).</p>\n<p data-b=\"131\">The stress test: loan losses jump by 3.2% of assets to 4% in a severe recession. Pre-tax operating income moves from +0.6% to −2.6% of assets; after 30% tax the loss is about 1.8% of assets. Equity falls from 5% to 3.2% and survives. If equity had been only 1% (Table 2.4, p. 30), one such year would wipe it out.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"132\"><strong>Quotable (p. 29):</strong> \"How much equity capital does DLC need? This question can be answered by hypothesizing an extremely adverse scenario and considering whether the bank would survive.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"133\"><strong>Use in the thesis.</strong> This is the simplest possible version of what your model does: shock loan losses, push them through the income statement, and see what is left of capital. You can cite it to justify the logic of a loss-shock design before moving to the richer methods in Sections 5 and 6.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"134\"><strong>Quotable (p. 30):</strong> \"Equity provides the best protection against adverse events\"; subordinated debt \"does not provide as good a cushion for the bank as equity because it does not prevent the bank’ s insolvency.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"135\" id=\"b135\">4.2 Risk-weighted assets and the Basel I to Basel II step (Chapter 15)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"136\">Basel I gave every corporate loan a 100% weight regardless of credit quality (p. 336: \"A loan to a corporation with a AAA credit rating is treated in the same way as one to a corporation with a B credit rating\"). Basel II made weights depend on ratings (standardised approach) or on the bank’ s own estimates (IRB).</p>\n<table data-b=\"137\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.11.</strong> Hull, Risk Management and Financial Institutions (4th ed., 2015): 4.2 Risk-weighted assets and the Basel I to Basel II step (Chapter 15)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Exposure (Basel II standardised, Table 15.4, p. 338)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">AAA to AA−</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">A+ to A−</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">BBB+ to BBB−</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">BB+ to B−</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Below B−</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Unrated</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Corporations</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">20%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">50%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">100%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">100 to 150%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">150%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">100%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"138\">Other Basel II weights quoted by Hull: retail 75%, residential mortgage 35%, commercial real estate 100% (p. 338). Worked example (Example 15.5, p. 339): $100m of A-rated corporate loans, $10m AAA government bonds and $50m of residential mortgages give RWA of 0.5×100 + 0×10 + 0.35×50 = $67.5m, against $125m under Basel I. Total capital must be 8% of RWA (equation 15.5, p. 337); a direct capital charge is multiplied by 12.5 to convert it to an RWA equivalent.</p>\n<p data-b=\"139\"><strong>The three pillars (p. 336 to 337, 346 to 347).</strong> Pillar 1 minimum capital; Pillar 2 supervisory review (banks must assess their own capital adequacy and supervisors may require more); Pillar 3 market discipline through disclosure. Pillar 2 expects banks to run credit-risk stress tests (p. 347).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"140\" id=\"b140\">4.3 The IRB capital formula, step by step (p. 340 to 343)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"141\">This is the most useful technical passage for a capital-adequacy thesis.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"142\"><strong>Idea.</strong> Capital is set so the bank survives a bad year with 99.9% confidence. \"The capital required is therefore the value at risk minus the expected loss\" (p. 340), because expected losses are already priced into loan interest.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"143\"><strong>Worst-case default rate</strong>, from a one-factor Gaussian copula with PD and correlation ρ (equation 15.6, p. 341):</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"144\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>ρ</mi></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.999</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math></p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"145\"><strong>Capital for the portfolio</strong> is the sum over loans of (equation 15.7, p. 341):</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"146\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>×</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"147\"><strong>Corporate exposures</strong> also use a correlation that falls as PD rises (equation 15.8, p. 342) and a maturity adjustment MA (equation 15.10, p. 343), so capital = EAD × LGD × (WCDR − PD) × MA (15.9, p. 342) and RWA = 12.5 × that amount (p. 343).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"148\">The table below (Table 15.6, p. 343) shows how WCDR responds to PD for corporate exposures. Read it as a sensitivity: a rise in PD of the sort a transition shock could cause raises the capital charge sharply.</p>\n<table data-b=\"149\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.12.</strong> Hull, Risk Management and Financial Institutions (4th ed., 2015): 4.3 The IRB capital formula, step by step (p. 340 to 343)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">PD</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">0.1%</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">0.5%</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">1%</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">1.5%</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">2.0%</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">WCDR</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3.4%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">9.8%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">14.0%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">16.9%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">19.0%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"150\">Under Foundation IRB, LGD is fixed at 45% for senior and 75% for subordinated claims, and maturity at 2.5 years in most cases (p. 343). <strong>Note:</strong> these parameters are from the 2015 edition; the Basel Framework (Section 2) has the current ones.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"151\"><strong>Quotable (p. 340):</strong> \"Regulators base the capital requirement on the value at risk calculated using a one-year time horizon and a 99.9% confidence level. They recognize that expected losses are usually covered by the way a financial institution prices its products.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"152\"><strong>Use in the thesis.</strong> If your model raises PD or LGD for carbon-intensive borrowers, equation 15.9 and Table 15.6 show how the regulatory capital requirement would rise. That is a clean, citable transmission from a climate-induced change in credit quality to the denominator of CRAR.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"153\" id=\"b153\">4.4 Basel III in Hull’ s account (Section 16.2, p. 357 to 361)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"154\">Total capital = Tier 1 equity + additional Tier 1 + Tier 2; \"There is no Tier 3 capital\" (p. 357).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"155\">Minimums: Tier 1 equity 4.5%, total Tier 1 6%, total 8% of RWA (p. 358). Compare Basel I: 2% and 4% (p. 358).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"156\">Conservation buffer: a further 2.5% in core equity; total requirement in normal times is 7% for equity, 8.5% for Tier 1 and 10.5% for total capital (p. 359).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"157\">Dividend limits as equity falls into the buffer (Table 16.2, p. 359): for example 80% of earnings must be retained when equity is between 5.125% and 5.75%.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"158\">Countercyclical buffer 0 to 2.5%, set by national authorities (p. 359).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"159\">Leverage ratio: 3% minimum in the 2015 text, based on Tier 1 over unweighted exposure (p. 360). Hull explains its rationale: regulators \"thought that banks had too much discretion in the way risk-weighted assets were calculated.\"</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"160\">These match the Basel Framework figures in Section 2 and give you the narrative behind them.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"161\" id=\"b161\">4.5 Scenario analysis and stress testing (Chapter 22, p. 463 to 475)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"162\">Hull’ s chapter reads like a design manual for a stress test.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"163\"><strong>Quotable (p. 463):</strong> \"Stress testing involves evaluating the impact of extreme, but plausible, scenarios that are not considered by value at risk (VaR) or expected shortfall (ES) models.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"164\"><strong>Ways to generate scenarios (p. 464 to 466).</strong> Stress one variable; replay a historical episode of several variables moving together; magnify history; or have senior management brainstorm \"What can go wrong?\" He prefers management-built scenarios, with a committee of three to five people with different backgrounds (p. 465). Climate scenarios are management-built in this sense, since they are not found in the historical record.</p>\n<p data-b=\"165\"><strong>Core and peripheral variables (p. 466).</strong> When only a few variables are stressed, the others should not just be held constant; forecast them conditional on the core shocks, using stressed correlations.</p>\n<p data-b=\"166\"><strong>Knock-on effects (p. 466 to 468).</strong> A scenario is incomplete if it ignores what happens when many institutions respond the same way. The LTCM case shows a stress test that was correct about the first-round shock and wrong about the second. Compare Borio et al. on contagion in Section 1.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"167\"><strong>Quotable (p. 466):</strong> Scenarios \"should include not only the immediate effect on the financial institution’ s portfolio of shock to market variables, but also any ’ knock-on’ effect resulting from many different financial institutions being affected by the shock in the same way and responding in the same way.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"168\"><strong>Reverse stress testing (p. 468).</strong> Search for scenarios that cause failure. This is one way to ask how large a transition shock would have to be to push a bank below its CRAR minimum.</p>\n<p data-b=\"169\"><strong>Using the results (p. 473).</strong> \"The biggest problem in stress testing is using the results effectively\"; managers tend to say \"there are always one or two scenarios that will sink us.\" Hull urges the better question: \"Are the risks associated with these scenarios acceptable?\" Hull also describes assigning probabilities to stress scenarios so they can be combined with VaR (p. 474), a device that may help you rank scenarios.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"170\" id=\"b170\">4.6 How this feeds the thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"171\"><strong>Mechanism:</strong> DLC (p. 28 to 30) and the IRB formula (p. 340 to 343) show how a loss shock and a PD shock reach capital.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"172\"><strong>Method:</strong> Chapter 22 gives vocabulary and design principles for your scenario analysis, and supports an honest limitations section.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"173\"><strong>Limit:</strong> a 2015 textbook; use it for mechanisms and cite RBI or Basel for current rules.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h1 data-b=\"174\" id=\"b174\">5. Bluhm, Overbeck and Wagner, An Introduction to Credit Risk Modeling (2nd ed., 2010)</h1>\n<p data-b=\"175\"><strong>Source:</strong> Bluhm, C., Overbeck, L. and Wagner, C. (2010), <em>An Introduction to Credit Risk Modeling</em>, 2nd edition, Chapman &amp; Hall/CRC (your file \"Bluhm, Overbeck &amp; Wagner, An Introduction to Credit Risk Modeling (Book).pdf\", 385 pages). Page references are the book’ s printed pages.</p>\n<p data-b=\"176\"><strong>What I read.</strong> From Chapter 1: expected loss, LGD, the PD-LGD-EAD dependence remark, unexpected loss, economic capital and the Basel section (pages 1 to 28 and 45 to 48). From Chapter 2: the CreditPortfolioView model (pages 71 to 77) and the start of the one-factor model (pages 80 to 87). I did not read the CreditRisk+ chapter, the asset value model chapter in full, risk contributions, the structured-credit chapters or the appendices. The book contains no climate content.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"177\" id=\"b177\">Why read it</h2>\n<p data-b=\"178\">This is the technical backbone for any model that moves a bank’ s loss and capital with the economy. Two ideas matter most for you: the split of loss into expected and unexpected parts, and the <strong>CreditPortfolioView</strong> framework, which makes default probabilities depend on macroeconomic variables. Replace \"macro variables\" with \"climate-transition variables\" and you have the structure of a climate-conditioned credit model.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"179\" id=\"b179\">Vocabulary</h2>\n<table data-b=\"180\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.13.</strong> Bluhm, Overbeck and Wagner, An Introduction to Credit Risk M: Vocabulary</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Term</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">PD / LGD / EAD</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Probability of default; fraction of exposure lost if default occurs; amount exposed at default (p. 3).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Expected loss (EL)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The average loss; priced into the loan and covered by provisions (p. 3 to 4).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Unexpected loss (UL)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The standard deviation of loss around the mean (p. 22).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Economic capital (EC)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A high quantile of the loss distribution minus expected loss (p. 27).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Loss distribution</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The probability distribution of portfolio loss; its shape fixes EL, UL and EC (p. 29).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Migration matrix</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Probabilities of moving between rating classes in a year, including default (p. 72).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Conditional vs unconditional</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Conditional: given the state of the economy; unconditional: averaged over states (p. 72).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">One-factor model</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">All obligors share one systematic factor Y with asset correlation ρ (p. 80).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"181\" id=\"b181\">5.1 Expected loss (Section 1.1, p. 2 to 4)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"182\">Each loan has a loss variable: exposure times loss fraction times a default indicator.</p>\n<p data-b=\"183\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mover><mi>L</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>×</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></p>\n<p data-b=\"184\">Its expectation is the expected loss. With EAD and LGD independent of default (Proposition 1.1.3, p. 4):</p>\n<p data-b=\"185\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow></math></p>\n<p data-b=\"186\">The authors treat EL as an insurance premium charged to borrowers and set aside as a reserve (p. 2). They add a caution: for banks under US accounting rules, loss reserves \"can not be used in the afore-mentioned sense of an expected loss reserve\", so the reserve is partly a theoretical concept (Remark 1.1.1, p. 2). The same applies in practice to provisioning rules in other countries.</p>\n<p data-b=\"187\"><strong>Connection to India (Section 6 will cover NPAs).</strong> Specific provisions and the PD-LGD-EAD triple are the technical language behind gross and net NPA ratios.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"188\" id=\"b188\">5.2 The independence assumption is wrong in a recession (Section 1.1.4, p. 21 to 22)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"189\">The simple formula for EL assumes PD, LGD and EAD are independent. The authors argue this fails exactly in bad times:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"190\">In a recession defaults rise, banks sell more collateral, collateral prices fall, recoveries fall, and so <strong>LGD rises with PD</strong>.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"191\">Firms in distress draw on open credit lines, so <strong>EAD rises with PD</strong> too.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"192\"><strong>Quotable (p. 21):</strong> \"defaults and recoveries to some extent are influenced by the same underlying systematic risk drivers so that they can not be independent.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"193\"><strong>Use in the thesis.</strong> A climate shock that cuts collateral values (stranded assets, flood-exposed property) is the same mechanism. It supports modelling a shock to LGD together with PD, rather than PD alone. The Basel real-estate FAQ on climate and property values (Section 2, p. 206) says the same in regulatory language.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"194\" id=\"b194\">5.3 Unexpected loss and economic capital (Sections 1.2, p. 22 to 29)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"195\"><strong>Unexpected loss</strong> is the standard deviation of the loss variable (Definition 1.2.1, p. 22). For one loan with deterministic EAD (Proposition 1.2.2, p. 22 to 23):</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"196\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>D</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>V</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>D</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>D</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"197\"><strong>Economic capital</strong> at confidence level α is the α-quantile of portfolio loss minus the portfolio’ s expected loss (Definition 1.2.8, equation 1.15, p. 27):</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"198\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"199\">With α = 99.98% the capital covers losses in 9,998 out of 10,000 years; it will fail in the other 2 (p. 28).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"200\">EL charges do not depend on the rest of the portfolio, but EC charges do: a loan that adds concentration needs more capital than the same loan in a diversified book (p. 28). This is the formal case for concentration risk, which a heavily exposed power, steel or cement book creates.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"201\">The regulatory IRB formula in Hull (Section 4.3) is this logic with α = 99.9% and a Gaussian one-factor model.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"202\"><strong>Quotable (p. 28):</strong> \"the EL charges are portfolio independent, but EC charges are portfolio dependent.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 data-b=\"203\" id=\"b203\">5.4 The Basel section (Section 1.3, p. 45 to 48)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"204\">The authors give a short history. Basel I applied a flat 8% to risk-weighted assets, with weights of 0% for governments, 20% for OECD banks and 100% for corporates \"no matter if the borrowing firm is a more or less risky obligor\" (p. 46). Basel II introduced risk-sensitive weights, and they restate the IRB formula (p. 47):</p>\n<p data-b=\"205\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12.5</mn><mo>×</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></p>\n<p data-b=\"206\">where K(PD) is the 99.9% quantile of the limiting loss distribution minus PD, with correlation ρ(PD) falling as PD rises (p. 47 to 48). They note that Basel II \"do<span>[</span>es<span>]</span> not allow the use of internal credit risk models for the calculation of regulatory capital\" (p. 47), only the prescribed formula. This matches Hull’ s account, and the Basel Framework (Section 2) is the current text.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"207\" id=\"b207\">5.5 The one-factor model: where correlation and the cycle enter (Section 2.5.1, p. 80 to 87)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"208\">All obligors’ asset returns share one systematic factor Y (the \"state of the economy\") plus an idiosyncratic part (equation 2.48, p. 80). Conditional on Y, the default probability of obligor i is (equation 2.49, p. 80):</p>\n<p data-b=\"209\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>ρ</mi></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>Y</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math></p>\n<p data-b=\"210\">A bad economy (low Y) raises everyone’ s default probability at once. For a large portfolio the share of defaults converges to this expression (equation 2.54, p. 87), which is what the Basel IRB formula evaluates at the 99.9% worst-case Y. If you build a stylised loss model, this is the standard way to turn a single systematic shock into a portfolio default rate.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"211\" id=\"b211\">5.6 CreditPortfolioView: macro variables drive defaults (Section 2.4.3, p. 71 to 77)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"212\">This section is the most directly useful for your thesis.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"213\"><strong>Quotable (p. 71 to 72):</strong> CreditPortfolioView (CPV) \"is a ratings-based portfolio model incorporating the dependence of default and migration probabilities on the economic cycle.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"214\"><strong>How it works</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"215\">Start with an average (unconditional) rating migration matrix M (p. 72).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"216\">Divide the economy into risk segments, such as industry groups, each reacting differently to the cycle (p. 73).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"217\">For each segment, simulate a conditional default probability p_s. Define a <strong>risk index</strong> r_s = p_s / (the segment’ s average default probability) (equation 2.41, p. 73).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"218\">Shift the migration matrix: m_ij(s) = α_ij (r_s − 1) + m_ij (equation 2.42, p. 73). If r_s &lt; 1 the economy is expanding and downgrades fall; if r_s &gt; 1 it is a recession and downgrades rise (p. 74 to 75).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"219\">Build a loss distribution for each scenario, then aggregate (p. 75).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"220\"><strong>Where macro variables enter (CPV Macro, p. 76 to 77).</strong> Each segment has an index driven by macro variables, such as \"the unemployment rate, the growth rate of the Gross Domestic Product (GDP), interest or currency exchange rates\" (p. 76):</p>\n<p data-b=\"221\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p>\n<p data-b=\"222\">The macro variables follow autoregressive processes (equation 2.45; Wilson used AR(2)), and the conditional default probability is a logit transform of the index (equation 2.46, p. 77):</p>\n<p data-b=\"223\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo>exp</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p>\n<p data-b=\"224\">An alternative, <strong>CPV Direct</strong>, skips the macro regression and draws segment default probabilities from a gamma distribution, which is easier to calibrate (p. 75 to 77). The authors note CPV can \"incorporate stressed tails in the systematic risk model ... in order to study the impact of heavy recessions\" (p. 75).</p>\n<p data-b=\"225\"><strong>Use in the thesis.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"226\">The structure (macro or policy variables → segment index → PD and migration → loss → capital) is the template for a <strong>transition-risk credit model</strong>. You would add a carbon-price or policy variable to X and let carbon-intensive sectors (power, steel, cement, oil and gas) be separate risk segments with their own coefficients.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"227\">The segment idea matches the sector-wise exposure data you will take from the RBI reports (Section 6).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"228\">The calibration difficulty the authors stress (many parameters, \"The calibration of CPV Macro is more complicated\", p. 75) is a limitation you can cite when you choose a simpler approach.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"229\" id=\"b229\">5.7 How this feeds the thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"230\"><strong>Concepts:</strong> EL, UL, EC and the loss distribution, with precise definitions you can cite.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"231\"><strong>Mechanism:</strong> PD, LGD and EAD move together in a downturn (p. 21), so shock them jointly.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"232\"><strong>Model template:</strong> CreditPortfolioView (p. 71 to 77) and the one-factor model (p. 80 to 87).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"233\"><strong>Limit:</strong> a 2010 text, written before the 2017 Basel changes; use it for modelling, not for current rules.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h1 data-b=\"234\" id=\"b234\">6. RBI, Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India (2007-08 to 2024-25)</h1>\n<p data-b=\"235\"><strong>Source:</strong> Reserve Bank of India, <em>Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India</em>, the annual statutory report submitted to the Central Government under Section 36(2) of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949 (cover text of each file). Your folder holds 18 PDFs, one per year, mostly under opaque file names. Page references below are <strong>printed page numbers</strong> of the edition named.</p>\n<p data-b=\"236\"><strong>What I read.</strong> From the 2024-25 edition (published December 2025): the capital adequacy, leverage, asset quality and sectoral credit passages and tables of Chapter IV, the climate passages in Chapters I and III, and one appendix table. From earlier editions I read only the passages cited: Basel II implementation (2007-08), Basel III phasing (2012-13), the NPA peak (2017-18 and 2018-19), and the climate and green-finance passages (2021-22, 2022-23, 2023-24). I did <strong>not</strong> read the other editions, the chapters on co-operative banks, NBFCs, payments or financial inclusion, or the appendix tables beyond the one cited. The report series is aggregate: it gives bank-group totals (PSBs, private banks and so on), not bank-by-bank data.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"237\" id=\"b237\">The files in your folder</h2>\n<table data-b=\"238\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.14.</strong> RBI, Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India (2007-: The files in your folder</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Edition (cover text)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Your file name starts with</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Pages</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2007-08 (year ended June 2008)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">88980.pdf</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">508</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2008-09</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RTP0809PRD_Full.pdf</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">413</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2009-10</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RTP081110FL.pdf</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">218</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2010-11</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0TPBI121111_FULL.pdf</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">215</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2011-12</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0TPB021112FLS.pdf</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">191</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2012-13</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0RTP21112013_F.pdf</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">189</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2013-14 (published as Financial Stability Report, December 2014, including the Trend and Progress chapters)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">FSR29122014_FL.pdf</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">88</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2014-15 (partial: 40 pages only)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0FLTP577BF4E...</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2015-16 (partial: 56 pages only)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0FRTP16A120A...</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">56</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2016-17</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0RTP20161778B7...</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">216</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2017-18</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0RTP2018_FE9E97...</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">199</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2018-19</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0RTP241219FL760D...</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">194</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2019-20 (cover reads \"year ended June 30, 2020\" but the title page says 2019-20)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0RTP2020_F3D078...</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">230</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2020-21</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0RTP2020CF9C9E...</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">248</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2021-22</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0RTP20212225730A...</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">234</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2022-23</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0RTP27122023D93943...</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">228</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2023-24</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0RTP261220247FFF1F...</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">228</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2024-25</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0RTP291220258C89B9...</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">220</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"239\"><strong>Two flags.</strong> The 2014-15 and 2015-16 files are short, so they are extracts, not full reports. The 2019-20 cover wording is RBI’ s own mismatch, not a file error. The covers of the older editions say \"year ended June 30\", which I take to reflect RBI’ s earlier July to June reporting year (my inference); the 2020-21 and later covers say \"year ended March 31\".</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"240\" id=\"b240\">Vocabulary</h2>\n<table data-b=\"241\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.15.</strong> RBI, Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India (2007-: Vocabulary</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Term</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SCB, PSB, PVB, FB, SFB, RRB</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Scheduled commercial banks; public sector, private, foreign, small finance and regional rural banks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">GNPA / NNPA ratio</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Gross (net) non-performing assets as a share of gross (net) advances.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">PCR</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Provision coverage ratio: provisions held against NPAs (here not adjusted for write-offs).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Slippage ratio</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">New NPAs in the year as a share of standard advances at the start of the year (p. 59, para IV.30).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SMA</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Special mention accounts: loans showing early stress before they become NPAs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RE</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Regulated entity, RBI’ s term for banks and other supervised institutions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RB-CRIS</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Reserve Bank Climate Risk Information System: a planned climate data repository.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"242\" id=\"b242\">6.1 Where the banks stand: 2024-25 snapshot</h2>\n<p data-b=\"243\">The 2024-25 edition reports figures at end-March 2025 unless noted.</p>\n<table data-b=\"244\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.16.</strong> RBI, Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India (2007-: 6.1 Where the banks stand: 2024-25 snapshot</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Indicator</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">PSBs</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">All SCBs</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Capital funds (₹ crore)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">13,55,864</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">31,78,386</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Table IV.5, p. 58</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Risk-weighted assets (₹ crore)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">84,23,011</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1,83,05,026</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Table IV.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">CRAR (%)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">16.1 (15.5 a year earlier)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">17.4 (16.9)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Table IV.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Tier 1 ratio (%)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">13.8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">15.5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Table IV.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Leverage ratio (%)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6.3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8.0</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Table IV.7, p. 59</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">GNPA ratio (%)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2.6 (3.5 a year earlier)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2.2 (2.7)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Table IV.9, p. 60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Net NPA ratio (%)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0.5 (0.8)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0.5 (0.6)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Table IV.9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Provision coverage ratio (%)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">78.5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">76.3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">para IV.22, p. 56</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Gross NPAs written off in 2024-25 (₹ crore)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">91,176</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1,57,563</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Table IV.9</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"245\"><strong>Quotable (IV.24, p. 57):</strong> \"In India, the minimum regulatory capital to risk-weighted assets ratio (CRAR) requirement for banks is set at 9.0 per cent <span>[</span>11.5 per cent inclusive of capital conservation buffer (CCB)<span>]</span> and Tier 1 capital ratio requirement at 7.0 per cent, both one percentage point above the Basel III norms.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"246\"><strong>Quotable (IV.29, p. 59):</strong> \"The GNPA ratio of SCBs declined further to a multi-decadal low of 2.2 per cent at end-March 2025 from 2.7 per cent at end-March 2024.\" The slippage ratio \"declined for the fifth consecutive year to 1.4 per cent\" (IV.30, p. 59).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"247\"><strong>Back-of-envelope headroom (my calculation, not RBI’ s).</strong> For PSBs, 11.5% of RWA is ₹9.69 lakh crore. Total capital of ₹13.56 lakh crore exceeds that by about ₹3.87 lakh crore, or 4.6 percentage points of RWA. For scale, the entire stock of PSB gross NPAs at end-March 2025 was ₹2.84 lakh crore (Table IV.9). This ignores tax, provisions already held, growth in RWA, and the fact that Tier 2 capital absorbs losses only in a gone-concern state. Use it as an order of magnitude to frame how large a transition shock would have to be, not as a result.</p>\n<p data-b=\"248\"><strong>Caution on the NPA decline.</strong> Of the reduction in SCB gross NPAs in 2024-25 (₹2.75 lakh crore), write-offs were ₹1.58 lakh crore, about 57%; recoveries and upgradations were 42.8% (para IV.29). Write-offs improve the GNPA ratio without recovering money, so treat \"multi-decadal low\" with care when you measure credit quality.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"249\" id=\"b249\">6.2 Where the credit goes (sectoral deployment)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"250\">Table IV.16 (p. 67) gives SCB credit by sector at end-March 2025 (total bank credit ₹1,82,43,972 crore):</p>\n<table data-b=\"251\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.17.</strong> RBI, Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India (2007-: 6.2 Where the credit goes (sectoral deployment)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Sector</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">₹ crore</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Share of bank credit (my calculation)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Agriculture and allied</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">22,87,060</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">12.5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Industry (micro, small, medium, large)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">39,85,660</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">21.8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">of which large industry</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">28,23,942</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">15.5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Services</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">50,93,565</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">27.9%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">of which commercial real estate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5,23,264</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2.9%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">of which NBFCs</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">16,35,102</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">9.0%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Personal loans</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">59,71,696</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">32.7%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">of which housing</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">30,10,477</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">16.5%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"252\">Three points matter for transition risk.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"253\"><strong>The report does not give a carbon-intensive breakdown.</strong> Industry is shown in size classes, and the industry-wise GNPA data in Charts IV.23 and IV.24 (p. 68 to 69) are graphics, not numbers I could extract. Sector-level exposure to power, steel, cement and oil and gas has to come from RBI’ s separate industry-wise credit releases or bank disclosures.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"254\"><strong>Lending to NBFCs is an indirect channel.</strong> Banks lent ₹16.35 lakh crore to NBFCs (Table IV.16). The same report’ s Appendix Table VI.5 (p. 188) shows that upper- and middle-layer NBFCs had ₹12.86 lakh crore of advances to power, 56% of their industry credit and about 27% of their gross advances (my calculation). A power-sector shock can therefore reach banks through NBFCs. I have not checked what the table’ s \"Power\" row includes.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"255\"><strong>Real estate is both a transition and a physical-risk channel.</strong> Housing is 16.5% of bank credit and commercial real estate 2.9%; the Basel FAQ on climate and property values (Section 2, p. 206) applies directly.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"256\" id=\"b256\">6.3 How we got here: Basel and the NPA cycle</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"257\"><strong>Basel II.</strong> Indian banks with foreign operations moved to the Basel II framework on 31 March 2008 and all other commercial banks (except RRBs and local area banks) by 31 March 2009; the minimum CRAR stayed at 9% (2007-08 edition, para 2.85, p. 44).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"258\"><strong>Basel III.</strong> \"The Basel III capital regulation has been implemented in India from April 1, 2013 in phases and will be fully implemented as on March 31, 2018\" (2012-13 edition, para 1.20, p. 7). RBI estimated that public sector banks would need additional capital of ₹4.15 trillion, of which ₹1.4 to 1.5 trillion would be equity (para 1.21).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"259\"><strong>The bad-loan cycle.</strong> The GNPA ratio of SCBs rose for seven consecutive years until 2018-19, as \"recognition of bad loans neared completion\" (2018-19 edition, IV.29, p. 50). For PSBs it reached 14.6% in 2017-18, with large borrowal accounts at 23.1% (2017-18 edition, IV.39 and IV.44, p. 60 to 61). Compare 2.6% in 2024-25.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"260\">This history is useful as an empirical benchmark: it shows how large an asset-quality shock Indian PSBs have already absorbed, and that the recovery relied on recapitalisation, the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code and write-offs rather than on organic capital generation. I read only the headline sentences for 2017-18 and 2018-19, so verify before you build an argument on the PSB recapitalisation amount.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"261\" id=\"b261\">6.4 What RBI says about climate risk, year by year</h2>\n<p data-b=\"262\">The series is the best single source for dating RBI’ s climate policy path. Each item below was read in the edition named.</p>\n<table data-b=\"263\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.18.</strong> RBI, Report on Trend and Progress of Banking in India (2007-: 6.4 What RBI says about climate risk, year by year</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Edition</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">What it records</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2021-22</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RBI joined the Network for Greening the Financial System on 23 April 2021; set up a Sustainable Finance Group in May 2021; surveyed banks in January 2022 (para III.26).</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">32</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2021-22</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Discussion paper on climate risk and sustainable finance released in July 2022, recommending that regulated entities embed climate risk in strategy, governance and risk management (para I.22).</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2021-22, Box III.2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Survey results, released 27 July 2022, covering 12 PSBs, 16 private banks and 6 foreign banks (see quotes below).</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">32</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2022-23</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Framework for green deposits issued 11 April 2023, with allocation to nine categories and third-party verification (para III.35).</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">35</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2023-24</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Draft disclosure framework on climate-related financial risks released 28 February 2024, built on four pillars (governance, strategy, risk management, metrics and targets) (para III.21, Box III.2).</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">33</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2023-24</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RBI is finalising a guidance note on scenario analysis and stress testing of climate risks, and plans RB-CRIS (paras I.34, I.35).</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2024-25</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RBI is \"following a building block approach\" to climate regulation; RB-CRIS operationalisation \"is underway\" (para III.47). RB-CRIS was announced in October 2024.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">38</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2024-25</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Climate finance section: RBI describes physical and transition risks that \"may affect credit, market, liquidity, and operational risks\" (para I.48).</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">10</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"264\"><strong>Quotable (2021-22 Box III.2, p. 32):</strong> \"Board-level engagement on climate risk and sustainable finance is inadequate.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"265\"><strong>Quotable (2021-22 Box III.2, p. 32):</strong> \"Most banks also felt that the available data were insufficient for an appropriate assessment of climate-related financial risks and the processes and methodologies to measure and monitor these risks were also not sufficiently developed.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"266\"><strong>Quotable (2021-22 Box III.2, p. 32):</strong> \"A majority of banks have not aligned their climate-related financial disclosures with any internationally accepted framework.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"267\"><strong>Quotable (2023-24, I.32, p. 7):</strong> \"Climate change risks are envisaged to impact profitability of financial institutions, growth prospects, and inflation dynamics and, thus, impinge upon financial stability and price stability.\" RBI adds that supervision needs \"enhanced risk management guidelines; disclosure requirements; periodic stress testing; and stipulating reasonable verification and assurance functions.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"268\"><strong>Quotable (2023-24, Box III.2, p. 33):</strong> \"Currently, REs are required to disclose information on material risks as a part of their Pillar 3 disclosures.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"269\"><strong>Quotable (2024-25, III.47, p. 38):</strong> \"The regulatory landscape for climate change risks is evolving considering the global developments as well as the maturity of the domestic ecosystem. In this regard, the Reserve Bank is following a building block approach, focusing on development of specific capacities and technical expertise in the REs.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p data-b=\"270\"><strong>What this tells you.</strong> As of the 2024-25 report, India’ s climate regime consists of disclosure and data initiatives, a green deposits framework and guidance in preparation. There is no climate-specific capital requirement, which matches the Basel Framework’ s position (Section 2) and the RBI capital circular (Section 3). That gap is your thesis’ s space.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"271\" id=\"b271\">6.5 How this feeds the thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"272\"><strong>Baseline data:</strong> PSB capital, NPA and provisioning levels at end-March 2025 (Tables IV.5, IV.9), for calibrating the starting point of a stress test.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"273\"><strong>Context for scenario size:</strong> the 2017-18 PSB GNPA peak of 14.6% shows a historical shock of known size.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"274\"><strong>Policy timeline:</strong> the dated climate milestones in Section 6.4, citable without needing the original circulars.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"275\"><strong>Limits:</strong> bank-group aggregates only; no carbon-intensive sector split; no bank-level data. For those you will need RBI’ s Statistical Tables Relating to Banks in India, sector-wise credit releases, or each bank’ s annual report (not in your folder).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h1 data-b=\"276\" id=\"b276\">7. Master quotables list and how Layer 3 feeds the thesis</h1>\n<h2 data-b=\"277\" id=\"b277\">7.1 Capital thresholds side by side</h2>\n<table data-b=\"278\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.19.</strong> Master quotables list and how Layer 3 feeds the thesis: 7.1 Capital thresholds side by side</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Requirement</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Basel (global)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">RBI (India)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Hull (2015 text)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Minimum CET1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4.5% (RBC20.1, p. 139)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5.5% (4.2.2(ii), p. 11)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4.5% (p. 358)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Minimum Tier 1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7% (p. 11)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Minimum total capital</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">9% (p. 11 to 12)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Conservation buffer</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2.5% CET1 (RBC30.2, p. 157)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2.5% CET1 (p. 12)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2.5% (p. 358)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Total capital plus conservation buffer</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">10.5%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">11.5% (p. 12)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">10.5% (p. 359)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Countercyclical buffer</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0 to 2.5% (RBC30.9, p. 160)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0 to 2.5% (17.2.1, p. 181)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0 to 2.5% (p. 359)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Leverage ratio</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3% in Hull’ s text; see the Framework’ s LEV chapter (not read)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4% D-SIBs, 3.5% others (16.2.2, p. 167)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3% (p. 360)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"279\">RBI’ s 2024-25 Trend and Progress report confirms the Indian figures as in force in 2025 (IV.24, p. 57). Because the RBI circular has been replaced (Section 3), re-check the Indian figures in the November 2025 Capital Adequacy Directions before quoting them as current.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"280\" id=\"b280\">7.2 The ten quotes to know</h2>\n<table data-b=\"281\">\n<caption><strong>Table 4.20.</strong> Master quotables list and how Layer 3 feeds the thesis: 7.2 The ten quotes to know</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">#</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote (short form)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Source and page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Use it for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The range of economic conditions or unexpected events that should be considered ... should include climate-related financial risks, both physical and transition risks.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Basel Framework, CRE36 FAQ, PDF p. 317</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Regulatory basis for putting transition risk in credit assessment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Banks \"should iteratively and progressively consider climate-related financial risks ... in their stress testing processes.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Basel Framework, CRE36 FAQ, PDF p. 322</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Legitimacy of a climate stress test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Add \"a margin of conservatism due to data deficiencies, such as poor data quality or scarce climate-related data.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Basel Framework, CRE36 FAQ, PDF p. 326</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Limitations section</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Banks should check whether \"the current market value incorporates the potential changes in the value of properties emerging from climate-related financial risks.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Basel Framework, CRE20 FAQ, PDF p. 206</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Collateral and LGD channel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"In India, the minimum ... CRAR ... is set at 9.0 per cent <span>[</span>11.5 per cent inclusive of capital conservation buffer<span>]</span> ... both one percentage point above the Basel III norms.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RBI T&amp;P 2024-25, IV.24, p. 57</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Defining the adequacy benchmark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The regulatory landscape for climate change risks is evolving ... the Reserve Bank is following a building block approach.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RBI T&amp;P 2024-25, III.47, p. 38</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Policy context and gap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Board-level engagement on climate risk and sustainable finance is inadequate.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RBI T&amp;P 2021-22, Box III.2, p. 32</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Evidence of low preparedness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"System looks strongest precisely when it is most vulnerable\" (paradox of financial instability).</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Borio, Drehmann and Tsatsaronis (2014), p. 8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Caution on reading 2025’ s strong capital ratios</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">9</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"How much equity capital does DLC need? ... hypothesizing an extremely adverse scenario and considering whether the bank would survive.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Hull, p. 29</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Simple logic of any capital stress test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">10</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"defaults and recoveries to some extent are influenced by the same underlying systematic risk drivers so that they can not be independent.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bluhm et al., p. 21</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Shocking PD and LGD together</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"282\"><strong>Check before use.</strong> Quote 8 is a paraphrase from my notes of Borio et al.; take the exact wording and page from Section 1. Quotes 1 to 4 are the Basel Committee’ s FAQ wording and are labelled by PDF page because the file has no printed page numbers.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"283\" id=\"b283\">7.3 How Layer 3 connects to the other layers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"284\"><strong>From Layer 1 and 2 (scenarios, discounting).</strong> Layer 3 supplies the receiving end of the chain: how a scenario’ s economic shock becomes a loss and then a capital ratio.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"285\"><strong>To Layer 4 (NGFS and climate stress tests).</strong> The Borio et al. warning about contagion models (Section 1) and the Basel position that climate is a driver of credit risk (Section 2) frame how to read the central-bank scenarios.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"286\"><strong>To Layer 6 (your model).</strong> Section 5 (CreditPortfolioView and the one-factor model) and Section 4.3 (the IRB formula) give the modelling skeleton; Section 6.1 gives the starting values for Indian PSBs.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"287\" id=\"b287\">7.4 The transmission chain the thesis tests</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"288\">A transition shock changes borrower cash flows and collateral values in carbon-intensive sectors.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"289\">Ratings, PDs and LGDs move together (Bluhm p. 21; Basel p. 317 and 326).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"290\">Provisions rise and NPAs are reclassified; risk weights on NPAs increase (RBI circular 5.12, p. 45).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"291\">Capital falls while RWA rises, so CRAR moves toward the 11.5% threshold (RBI, p. 12).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"292\">The cushion available is large in 2025 (PSB CRAR 16.1%, Section 6.1), so the question becomes how large a shock is needed, and whether the strong starting position hides vulnerability (Borio et al., p. 8).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"293\" id=\"b293\">7.5 What I did not read, and flags</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"294\"><strong>Replaced document:</strong> the RBI Basel III Master Circular (1 April 2025) in your folder is marked withdrawn; RBI’ s list shows new Capital Adequacy Directions dated 28 November 2025. I could not open the new text, so confirm figures and paragraph numbers there.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"295\"><strong>Basel Framework (1,429 pages):</strong> I read the capital minimums, buffers, IRB opening chapters and all climate FAQs. Not read: capital definition details, market and operational risk, securitisation, counterparty risk, liquidity, large exposures and the supervisory review chapters.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"296\"><strong>Hull (743 pages):</strong> four chapters or sections of 28 chapters. <strong>Bluhm (385 pages):</strong> Chapter 1, the CreditPortfolioView section and the start of the one-factor model.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"297\"><strong>RBI Trend and Progress (18 files):</strong> the 2024-25 edition in detail; other years only at cited passages. 2014-15 and 2015-16 are partial files.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"298\"><strong>Not in your folder but needed later:</strong> bank-level capital and NPA data, sector-wise credit to carbon-intensive industries, and the new RBI Capital Adequacy Directions.</p></li>\n</ul>\n","s":[[69,"b1"],[70,"b30"],[74,"b55"],[75,"b62"],[75,"b68"],[76,"b72"],[77,"b79"],[77,"b84"],[78,"b89"],[78,"b95"],[79,"b101"],[80,"b106"],[80,"b112"],[81,"b117"],[81,"b122"],[82,"b129"],[82,"b135"],[83,"b140"],[84,"b153"],[84,"b161"],[85,"b170"],[85,"b174"],[86,"b181"],[86,"b188"],[87,"b194"],[87,"b203"],[88,"b207"],[88,"b211"],[89,"b229"],[89,"b234"],[91,"b242"],[92,"b249"],[92,"b256"],[93,"b261"],[94,"b271"],[94,"b276"],[94,"b277"],[95,"b280"],[96,"b283"],[96,"b287"],[96,"b293"]]};